\section{Comparison with Existing Frameworks}

\subsection{Geometric foundations and the wider programme}
Papers I--III establish a minimal geometric and dynamical layer: projective
compatibility, controlled assignments, and witness-relative null exclusion.
Their hypotheses determine the mathematical conclusions. Cognitive models and
experimental contracts are introduced in subsequent layers, rather than
being premises supplied retrospectively to this geometric core.

\begin{center}\small
\begin{tabular}{|p{0.22\linewidth}|p{0.31\linewidth}|p{0.34\linewidth}|}
\hline
Question & Papers I--III & Subsequent layers \\ \hline
Mathematical object & Compatible states and metric assignments & Joint operational certificates and matched comparisons \\
Prediction & Conditional bounds and counterexamples & Intervention predictions in IV/VI and adjudication contracts in X \\
Indexed organization & No cognitive architecture is required by the geometric hypotheses & VII/VIII and the theoretical dependencies paper specify indexed control, ownership and conditional self-reference \\
Phenomenal association & Open bridge hypothesis & Remains open under additional identification and empirical criteria \\ \hline
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

\subsection{Integration, global availability, and higher-order representation}
Tononi's 2004 formulation links consciousness to information integration
\cite{tononi2004}. The structural assignment in this paper has a different
domain and codomain from that integration quantity. The distinction concerns
the objects being compared; it supplies neither a conversion between the
measures nor a verdict on the competing research programme.

Baars's global-workspace account concerns a cognitive architecture
\cite{baars1988}. The geometric core leaves such architecture unspecified;
later operational criteria can be applied to a specified architecture once
its states, controls and observation maps are identified.

Rosenthal's higher-order account relates a state's consciousness to suitable
higher-order awareness of that state \cite{rosenthal1986}. Indexed control
and ownership in the later QICN layers are operational constructions with
their own intervention contracts. Their relation to that higher-order
condition is a separate modelling question. The exact self-reference
operator in the theoretical dependencies paper retains its additional
hypotheses and its explicit obstructions.

\subsection{Mathematical conclusions and bridge hypotheses}
The phenomenal interpretation remains open in the sense of an independent
bridge hypothesis \cite{chalmers}. A faithful realization of a mathematical
model and an explanation of experience answer different questions. The
later prediction and adjudication layers specify how empirical evidence
could constrain a realization and its interpretation without altering the
proofs of the geometric results.

\section{Limitations and Open Problems}

\subsection{Limitations}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{No claim about qualia types, contents, or subjectivity.}
\item \textbf{No claim that all non-CCR systems are null.}
\item \textbf{Result is structural necessity, not phenomenological doctrine.}
\item \textbf{All results are conditional on the axioms of Papers I--II.}
\item \textbf{The physical existence of CCR systems is unknown.}
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Open Problems}

\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Characterization of $\Eplus$:} What is the full structure of positive regimes?
\item \textbf{Regime dynamics:} How do regimes evolve under channel evolution?
\item \textbf{Physical CCR:} Do CCR systems exist in physics (quantum gravity, etc.)?
\item \textbf{Biological CCR:} Could biological systems approximate CCR?
\item \textbf{Computational bounds:} Sharper non-simulability results?
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Foundation-Level Gaps and Non-Claims}\label{sec:gap-nonclaims}

The following gaps remain open in the current corpus and are documented here to prevent over-reading of the separation theorem.

\begin{nontheorem}[Topology consistency gap]\label{nonthm:topology}
Papers I--II assume both compact Hausdorff and Polish structures on state spaces, but do not prove their consistency for every QICN instantiation. The inverse limit $\mathcal{I}$ uses compactness and closed-subspace arguments, while the metric $d_w$ uses a metric regularity structure. Every compact metric space is Polish, so the composition is consistent when each $S_t$ is compact metric. Non-compact Polish spaces such as $\mathbb{R}^n$ require additional hypotheses and are not automatically covered.
\end{nontheorem}

\begin{nontheorem}[Channel--projection composition gap]\label{nonthm:channel-projection}
The observable channel $\mathcal{C}$ and the projective projections $\pi_{t+1\to t}:S_{t+1}\to S_t$ are not yet connected by a fully explicit composition law. Any detectability theorem that moves from observed responses to inverse-limit deformation requires such a composition. At present, that composition is an assumption to be specified for each concrete protocol, not a derived universal theorem.
\end{nontheorem}

\begin{nontheorem}[Null-regime exclusion requires external witness]\label{nonthm:null-external}
The instability theorem (Theorem~\ref{thm:instability}) requires a separated external witness $(z_0,z_1,\epsilon,C)$. CCR alone does not produce such a witness. The theorem is witness-relative: if a CCR system has a typed external witness with lower Lipschitz constant $C>0$, then both endpoints cannot simultaneously be null. It does not state that CCR by itself implies non-nullity.
\end{nontheorem}

\begin{nontheorem}[Estimator verification gap]\label{nonthm:estimator-gap}
No Lipschitz constant $K_i$, fiber oscillation $\omega_i(y)$, estimation error bound $\varepsilon_i$, or decision margin $\Delta^*$ has been computed for any QICN invariant. The projection-invariant bridge theorem is conditional on these quantities being verified, and all four material bridge hypotheses remain unverified.
\end{nontheorem}

