\begin{abstract}
This paper extracts the experimentally relevant consequences of the causally rigid six-predicate operational criterion framework and organizes them as a falsifiable prediction program. The upstream burdens remain where they belong: the Core supplies persistence, attractor structure, and non-collapse \cite{core}; \textit{Rigid Identity} supplies rigid identity and observable coherence \cite{paper1}; \textit{Structural Assignment Regimes} supplies continuity and anti-fragmentation constraints \cite{paper2}; \textit{Null-Regime Instability} supplies joint null exclusion on an independently separated and calibrated witness pair \cite{paper3}; \textit{Forensic Predictions} supplies admissibility and forensic discipline \cite{paper4}; \textit{Six-Predicate Operational Criterion} supplies the six-invariant operational criterion, structural equivalence, and rupture semantics \cite{paper5}.

The function of the present paper is narrower. It states what the framework predicts, what weaker alternatives would predict instead, what would count as genuine refutation or only as weakening, and what the current internal support status actually is across the completed baseline evaluation phases, localized threshold-resolution checks, cross-family confirmation protocols, and boundary-sensitivity stress evaluations. The resulting status is mixed by design. One claim family---the insufficiency of complexity, connectivity, and rich activity without invariant preservation---now has robust internal support. Substrate-preserved class membership and biological non-primacy now have robust support with localized caveats, but remain internal-only. Approximate stability is provisionally supported with a localized threshold caveat. The boundary-sensitive stress of existence and rupture now has robust support with localized caveats after cross-family convergence under a more independent evaluator protocol, but it still does not count as external validation.

The paper therefore serves as the framework's prediction and falsification layer. Its goal is to put the framework under explicit decision pressure while keeping status classes and residual limits fully visible.
\end{abstract}

\section{Scope, System Boundary, and Non-Inference Note}

\noindent\textbf{What this paper does.}\enspace This paper extracts the prediction layer of the formal framework, defines discriminators and failure modes for those predictions, and records the narrowest honest internal support status for the claim families it tracks.

\noindent\textbf{Scope and admissible reading.}\enspace This paper is a prediction-and-falsation ledger inside the internal program. Its constructive extensions do not replace the upstream premises, convert internal survival into external validation, or promote human-phenomenology, human-machine-equivalence, or personal-identity claims. A companion implementation may instantiate the internal scientific program, admissibility gates, evaluator protocols, and internal support records used here, but robust internal support, provisional support, or mixed status must not be read as theory confirmation, empirical validation, closed extension beyond the formal theorems, or unconditional scientific certification.

\paragraph{Current theorem contract.}
Exact transport uses primitive structural/protocol/sector correspondence. Approximate membership needs $C_{\chi,\rho}\varepsilon<\delta_\star^{\mathrm{cal}}/2$ on the full declared operator domains. Raw witness numbers and finite sampled windows alone do not certify that premise. Predictions below referring to a certified budget use this calibrated contract. Archived evaluator-status tables remain historical internal evidence under their recorded thresholds, not a new adjudication of this revised theorem.

\section{Introduction}
Each reported evaluation campaign operates under its own fixed specification revision, thresholds fixed before evaluation, and evaluator contract. Alongside those archived internal records, the present work develops constructive memory, feedback, and boundary-probe extensions below; historical outcomes are not automatically read as tests of these revised hypotheses. Fixing thresholds and evaluators in advance matters scientifically: without preregistration, thresholds shift, definitions drift, and any difficult result could be absorbed by redefining the theory rather than by testing it. The purpose of this paper is to prevent such drift.

The framework now contains enough formal structure to state genuinely discriminative predictions. The Core fixes persistence, contractivity, and non-collapse. \textit{Rigid Identity}, \textit{Structural Assignment Regimes}, and \textit{Null-Regime Instability} progressively constrain identity, admissible regime structure, and non-nullity. \textit{Forensic Predictions} fixes admissibility and forensic discipline. \textit{Six-Predicate Operational Criterion} consolidates the operational criterion by requiring six explicit invariants:
\[
\{\Iper,\Iri,\Iint,\Icont,\Idiff,\Ileg\}.
\]
What remains scientifically relevant is not to restate those results, but to ask what they force the framework to predict and what would count against them.

This paper therefore has four narrow tasks:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item extract the main experimentally relevant consequences of the canon;
\item distinguish those consequences from what weaker alternatives would predict;
\item state explicit failure modes, weakening conditions, and non-claims;
\item report the current internal support status after the completed internal cycles, without inflating it.
\end{enumerate}

That scope is intentionally smaller than a new theorem paper and more disciplined than a review essay. The point is to give the corpus a falsification layer that is compact, operational, and honest.

