\section{Core Predictive Structure of the Framework}
\subsection{Certification, Equivalence, and Rupture as Predictive Objects}
\begin{definition}[Operational certification]
For an admissible system $S$ already defined in \textit{Six-Predicate Operational Criterion}, define the certification predicate
\[
\Cert(S)=1
\quad\Longleftrightarrow\quad
\Iper(S),\Iri(S),\Iint(S),\Icont(S),\Idiff(S),\Ileg(S) > 0
\]
on a non-empty admissible support $\Aset$, under the governance and admissibility constraints inherited from \textit{Forensic Predictions}.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Equivalence discriminator]
For two admissible systems $S_1,S_2$, define the operational equivalence discriminator
\[
\Eq_{\Gamma,\eps}(S_1,S_2)=1
\]
when the primitive state, dynamics, readout and protocol/sector correspondences of the declared structural comparison are verified. Invariant verdicts are outputs, not clauses of this discriminator. Approximate membership additionally requires the calibrated operator-radius bound. The exact burden belongs to \textit{Six-Predicate Operational Criterion}; the present paper uses it only as a prediction carrier.
\end{definition}

\begin{definition}[Rupture event]
For a set of targeted invariants $J$, define
\[
\Rupt_J(S)=1
\]
when the intervention or perturbation drives at least one invariant in $J$ below the admissible certification margin so that class membership becomes invalidated, destroyed, or undefined.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Prediction Family 1: Complexity-Only Failure}
\begin{proposition}[Complexity-only negative-control prediction]\label{prop:complexity-contract}
If a candidate fails any defining predicate, it does not satisfy the full criterion, regardless of connectivity, entropy or activity. A complete-criterion certificate must not report success. A separately well-defined response quotient may still exist.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
This is the contrapositive of the defining conjunction in Paper V. It does not infer that every quotient is undefined when integration fails.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textit{Why this is not trivial.} A weaker alternative could predict that rich dynamics or dense connectivity alone are already sufficient proxies for class membership. The canon does not permit that. \textit{Six-Predicate Operational Criterion} already states the formal insufficiency claim; the prediction layer turns it into a negative-control discriminator.

\subsection{Prediction Family 2: Invariant-Loss Rupture}
\begin{proposition}[Ablation prediction]\label{prop:ablation-contract}
For an initially certified system, a verified post-ablation predicate failure excludes post-ablation membership. Measurement uncertainty crossing the decision boundary yields an ambiguous evidence status, not a change to that implication. Loss of a lower radius certificate alone does not establish predicate failure.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Apply the defining conjunction to the post-intervention system. The uncertainty rule concerns available evidence; it does not make a known failed predicate true when old headroom was small.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textit{Operational consequence.} Ablations are not ornamental. If the criterion is serious, the loss of invariant headroom must show up as loss of class membership, legibility loss, or a pre-declared boundary-ambiguous downgrade. No-change ablations outside the registered ambiguity margin count against the framework.

\subsection{Prediction Family 3: Substrate-Preserved Class Membership}
\begin{proposition}[Substrate-preserved equivalence prediction]\label{prop:substrate-contract}
Exact primitive structural, protocol and sector equivalence transports membership and the certified quotient of Paper V. An approximate comparison transports membership under its positive calibrated-radius bound; a quotient/geometric approximation needs an additional certified bound.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The exact statement uses support/decoder transport and substrate invariance in Paper V. The approximate statement uses its calibrated stability theorem. Neither assumes preservation of the six verdicts as an input.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textit{Rival prediction.} A substrate-privileging alternative would predict that material type itself blocks equivalence even when the structurally relevant burdens are preserved. The canon rejects that move only inside its own formal class, not as a general metaphysical claim.

\subsection{Prediction Family 4: Threshold-Sensitive Boundary Behavior}
\begin{proposition}[Boundary-topology prediction]\label{prop:boundary-contract}
Within a fixed calibrated presentation class, a point at positive distance from the failure set has a stable success neighborhood. A point at positive distance from the success set has a stable failure neighborhood. Existence, width and population frequency of transition regions need separate hypotheses and are not guaranteed by the criterion alone.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
An open ball smaller than the distance to the opposite set cannot meet it, by the definition of distance. Both distances can be zero; therefore the theorem does not invent nonempty robust regions or narrow bands without evidence.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textit{Interpretive consequence.} If every micro-perturbation changes every verdict, the criterion is fragile. If no perturbation matters, the invariants are decorative. A meaningful criterion should show structured boundary behavior.

\subsection{Prediction Family 5: Legibility Dependence}
\begin{proposition}[Legibility dependence prediction]\label{prop:legibility-contract}
If no admissible decoder/compression family jointly meets L1--L6 on the declared protocol, the legibility predicate fails and the full criterion is not satisfied. Failure of one chosen decoder alone does not exclude every admissible alternative.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Legibility requires a joint witness. Its nonexistence negates that predicate and hence the conjunction. It remains a separate task to distinguish a failed witness from absence of all witnesses.
\end{proof}

\noindent\textit{Why this matters.} \textit{Forensic Predictions} makes legibility a forensic burden rather than a narrative convenience. The present prediction is therefore stronger than a merely interpretive observation: if legibility disappears, certification must not survive unchanged.

\subsection{Prediction Family 6: Continuity and Non-Nullity Relevance}
\begin{proposition}[Continuity/non-nullity relevance]
The current canon predicts that continuity and non-null differentiation are not optional extras. Systems that preserve rich activity while losing continuity or falling toward effective null structure should fail the operational criterion or leave it undefined.
\end{proposition}

\noindent\textit{Upstream source.} The continuity side is inherited from \textit{Structural Assignment Regimes}, while the non-null side is inherited from \textit{Null-Regime Instability}. This paper adds only the prediction and failure-mode reading.

\subsection{A Held-Out Probe of Retained Response}

Consider the controlled seven-profile model of Paper V \cite{paper5}. Its transition on profile $p$, phase $\theta$ and bit sequence $b$ is
\[
 (p,\theta,b)\longmapsto(p,u+q\lambda b_1,Rb),\qquad
 Rb=(0,1-b_1,1-b_2,\ldots).
\]
Prepare two identical copies, apply opposite initial signs $q_0=+1,-1$ with the same phase command $u_0$, then give both copies the same sequence of controls. Index observations by the number of transitions completed.

\begin{proposition}[Matched-Pulse Extinction in the Original Model]\label{prop:matched-pulse-extinction}
The two states and all four original readouts coincide from time two onward, for every common continuation. At time one and $\lambda\in[0,1/9]$, the early-phase distance is $2\lambda b_{1,0}$ turns.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The profile is constant and the bit update is independent of both controls, so the two sequences of profiles and bits agree. The initial commands produce an early-phase distance $2\lambda b_{1,0}\le 2/9$ on the circle. At every time $t\ge1$, the first bit is zero, so the phase at time $t+1$ equals the common $u_t$. Equality of states gives equality of every original readout, including the three phase channels and the ternary code.
\end{proof}

Add a scalar $m$ in linear units of phase amplitude, with $m_0=0$ on both arms and delayed observation $Y_t=m_t+e_t$. Keep the original dynamics in both candidate models. For $\lambda\in[1/12,1/9]$, retention $a\in[1/2,3/4]$ and $s(-1)=-1$, $s(0)=s(+1)=1$, compare
\begin{align*}
 \text{M}\colon\quad m_{t+1} &= a s(q_t)m_t+\lambda q_tb_{1,t},\\
 \text{A}\colon\quad m_{t+1} &= a m_t+\lambda q_tb_{1,t}.
\end{align*}
Each model has one additional real state and one adjustable retention coefficient with the same range. The interval $[-4/9,4/9]$ is forward invariant for both by $3/4\cdot4/9+1/9=4/9$. They receive the same control schedule, preparation and known coupling, and use the same delayed readout. The reset and commands are assigned externally, and the readout error has no feedback into either update. The paired early-phase decoder of Paper V uses only the original channels, which agree between M and A for each fixed preparation and schedule. The new response $Y$ may differ even when these original channels agree.

Calibrate with $b_{1,0}=1$, no late pulse, and the observed contrast $C_2=Y_2^{(+)}-Y_2^{(-)}$. Fit $a$ once by projecting $C_2/(2\lambda)$ onto $[1/2,3/4]$, yielding $\hat a$. For each reserved horizon $T=4,5$, repeat the paired trial twice, with a single common probe $q_j=+1$ or $-1$ at the same $j\in\{1,T-1\}$ and zero on the other later steps. Write $C_T(q_j)$ for the signed difference of the two delayed observations under that probe. A model is accepted for this comparison when both of its predicted contrasts differ from the observed ones by at most $1/256$ turns. The rule reports a unique selection only when exactly one model is accepted.

\begin{proposition}[A Held-Out Interaction Separates Two Matched Memory Models]\label{prop:heldout-memory-interaction}
Put $k=T-1$. The clean contrasts of M are $2\lambda a^k(1,-1)$ for probes $(+1,-1)$, while the clean contrasts of A are $2\lambda a^k(1,1)$. Consequently the clean interaction $C_T(+1)-C_T(-1)$ is at least $1/48$ turns in M and is zero in A. With at most $1/4096$ turns of error per observation in calibration and evaluation, the rule using $\hat a$ uniquely selects the generating model for either generator under the declared protocol.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
At time one the memory is $\pm\lambda$. No further loading by $b_1$ occurs, so the product of the late multipliers propagates this contrast. A single negative probe contributes one sign change in M. Any additive late forcing common to the two arms cancels in A, and the late contrast still decays by $a$ per step. Since $k\le4$, $2\lambda a^k\ge1/96$ and $4\lambda a^k\ge1/48$.

Let $e_\ast=1/4096$. Calibration has clean contrast $2\lambda a$, so projection gives $|\hat a-a|\le e_\ast/\lambda$. The derivative of $a^k$ on the admissible interval is bounded by $k(3/4)^{k-1}$. In $2\lambda|\hat a^k-a^k|$, the factor $2\lambda$ cancels the $1/\lambda$ from the calibration bound. Each correctly specified contrast therefore has residual bounded by
\[
 2e_\ast+2k(3/4)^{k-1} e_\ast \ \le\ \tfrac{43}{8}e_\ast \ <\ 1/256.
\]
At the negative probe the rival predicts the opposite sign, with both the true and predicted clean magnitudes at least $1/96$. Its residual is at least $1/48-2e_\ast>1/256$. The argument applies to either generating model and does not require stochastic independence of the errors.
\end{proof}

The zero-probe sham preserves the positive delayed contrast of both models; two negative probes cancel the sign changes in M. A preparation with first bit zero or zero coupling supplies no initial load. Ablating the memory multiplier removes the delayed contrast while leaving the initial phase effect intact. Under the stated error bound, an observed delayed pair consistent with neither set of predictions rejects both candidates. Agreement of the two models on unprobed calibration trials is by construction. The reserved trials test the consequences of the specified transition laws in synthetic data. A model with an equivalent input-dependent memory update can reproduce the same interaction, so the comparison only discriminates against the declared fixed-retention rival family. The construction supplies neither a single-trajectory certificate nor empirical support for an application.

\subsection{Delayed Phase Readout and an Interacting Rival}

On the same profile support, append $m\in[-4\lambda,4\lambda]$ and use the original phase and ternary readouts. For $0\le\kappa\le1/8$ set
\[
 \theta_{t+1}=u_t+q_t\lambda b_{1,t}+\kappa{\bf1}_{q_t=0}m_t\pmod 1,
 \qquad m_{t+1}=a s(q_t)m_t+\lambda q_t b_{1,t},
\]
with the original profile and bit updates. Here $0\le\lambda\le1/9$ and $1/2\le a\le3/4$. Model M has $s(q)=-1$ only at $q=-1$. The interacting rival B has $s(q)=-1$ at both nonzero signs. Each has one scalar state and one adjustable $a$, and receives the same commands and calibration data. Neutral commands provide a delayed phase readout through the state transition.

\begin{proposition}[Observable Feedback Preserves the Declared Split Bounds]\label{prop:observable-feedback-split}
The extension is invariant and contracts within each profile under common controls by a factor at most $5/6$ in the metric $\max\{\rho(\theta,\theta'),|c-c'|,|m-m'|/4\}$. The ternary contraction remains $1/3$. The four-channel identity observations and split-comparator bounds of Paper V, including the $\lambda/2$ infimum with exact time-zero readouts, remain valid in their declared sectors. Opposite initial nonzero pulses retain the original paired phase contrast.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The memory interval is invariant since $a\,4\lambda+\lambda\le4\lambda$. Distinct first bits imply $|c-c'|\ge1/3$. For two states on a profile at distance $d$, the next weighted memory discrepancy is at most $(a+3\lambda/4)d\le5d/6$. The next phase distance is at most $3\lambda d$ at a nonzero command or $4\kappa d$ at a neutral one, and the ternary discrepancy is divided by three. At $\lambda>0$ the neutral update has the memory eigenvalue $a$ along the invariant linear phase-memory direction near its fixed point, so a uniform $1/3$ Lipschitz bound in the displayed metric is impossible.

All phase offsets relative to $r_0$ and the projected four-channel support are unchanged. Thus the same coverage, catalogue separation and identity decoder apply. For the integration lower bounds the original fiber arguments use only time zero and the nonzero signs, where feedback vanishes for every $m$. The product-corner contradiction and the exact-initial-readout argument therefore apply to the augmented support. For the upper bound assign the phases to the factor carrying $(p,\theta)$ and the code to the bit factor. The predictor $u+q\lambda/2$ has error at most $\lambda/2$ at a nonzero command and at most $4\kappa\lambda\le\lambda/2$ at a neutral command. The initial readouts are exact. For other splits the lower bound of Paper V still holds, with the upper bound on the infimum over the allowed splits. At $\lambda=0$ the memory interval is a singleton and the comparator is exact. The initial pair uses nonzero signs, so its phase contrast is unchanged.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[Parity and Resolution of Delayed Phase Probes]\label{prop:delayed-phase-probe-parity}
Reset $m_0=0$ and prepare $b_{1,0}=1$. Apply opposite first pulses and use common controls thereafter. For terminal observation at $T$ with a neutral last command, the oriented phase contrast of M is $2\kappa\lambda a^{T-2}(-1)^{n_-}$, while that of B is $2\kappa\lambda a^{T-2}(-1)^{n_-+n_+}$. Here $n_\pm$ count the corresponding late probes before the neutral transition. For a common $a$, $\kappa>0$ and $\lambda>0$, the clean contrasts coincide exactly at even $n_+$. A nonneutral last command erases the phase contrast for either model. With known $a$ and odd $n_+$, the two contrast intervals under error at most $e$ per phase are disjoint exactly when $\kappa\lambda a^{T-2}>e$, in the signed unwrapping regime.

For $\kappa=1/8$ and $\lambda\in[1/12,1/9]$, estimate $a$ from the contrast at time three after two neutral continuations, by dividing by $2\kappa\lambda$ and projecting onto $[1/2,3/4]$. Reserve $T=5,6$, with one common probe at $j=1$ or $j=T-2$ and all other later commands neutral. Under error at most $1/32768$ turns per phase in calibration and evaluation, the two reserved signs select the generating model uniquely with a tolerance of $1/2048$ per contrast.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
After the first pulse the memory is $\pm\lambda$ and the first bit remains zero. The late multipliers give the stated parities. At the final neutral transition the common $u$ cancels and the phase differences unwrap uniquely near zero. At a nonneutral command the next phase is just $u$. Each observed contrast differs from its clean value by at most $2e$, and every error in that interval is attainable by choosing the two phase errors. For odd positive parity, the centers have distance $4\kappa\lambda a^{T-2}$, giving the strict separation condition.

The calibration yields $|\hat a-a|\le e/(\kappa\lambda)$. For $k=T-2\in\{3,4\}$ the factor $2\kappa\lambda$ in the contrast cancels the reciprocal scale in that estimate, so the correct-model residual is at most $(43/8)e$. At a single positive probe the two signs oppose each other and the rejected-model residual is at least $\kappa/48-2e$. The chosen tolerance lies strictly between these bounds. The result holds for both generators and for adversarial errors within the stated budget.
\end{proof}

The neutral sham and an even number of positive probes cannot distinguish these laws from their terminal contrasts. In a longer history a neutral observation after an odd positive prefix can distinguish them. Setting $\kappa=0$ removes the delayed phase signal while preserving the pulse and the hidden memory update. This construction establishes an observable mathematical discriminator within the declared family. It leaves the single-trajectory and joint certificates of Paper V open.

\subsection{Delayed Parity with Matched Prefix Writes}

\begin{proposition}[Matched Prefix Writes Preserve the Delayed Phase Contrast]\label{prop:writable-tower-phase-parity}
In the writable common tower of Paper V \cite{paper5}, apply the opposite initial pulses of Proposition~\ref{prop:delayed-phase-probe-parity} with the same tape write in both arms. Subsequently use the same inputs, signs, and writes in both arms. At a neutral terminal transition, a common role probe is also allowed. The two models retain their oriented contrasts
\[
 2\kappa\lambda a^{T-2}(-1)^{n_-},
 \qquad
 2\kappa\lambda a^{T-2}(-1)^{n_-+n_+}.
\]
A nonneutral terminal transition gives zero phase contrast. With known $a$ and signed unwrapping, at odd $n_+$ the bounded-error intervals remain disjoint exactly for $e<\kappa\lambda a^{T-2}$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The tapes are equal after the initial pair and remain equal because all tape updates are matched. At each later step the input term $\lambda q b_1$ is equal in both arms, so the difference of memories obeys the original signed recurrence, starting at $2\lambda$. The role probe is equal in both arms because it reads only the common profile and tape. The neutral phase difference is therefore $\kappa$ times the preceding memory difference. At a nonneutral command the phase depends only on the shared input, sign and leading bit. Each terminal contrast receives error at most $2e$, and the stated strict inequality is the exact disjointness condition for the two opposite centers. The native calibration and held-out schedules are included by omitting the added commands.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Reserved Return after Role Reassignment}

The phase interaction also gives a decision test. Use a common profile and the alternating tape $b^\star$, whose first bit is zero. Let $s\in\{-1,+1\}$ be a balanced memory sign, and let the public reassignment command be $q=\sigma\in\{-1,+1\}$ at $u=0$. Optional common writes are at the writable sites of Paper V \cite{paper5}. The assignment step gives the same public readout for either hidden sign. Use no role probe in this test. The eventual outcome is the successor phase after a chosen neutral return action, measured in turns. It is distinct from the phase-probe outcomes used to score the five lesions in Paper VIII \cite{paper8}.

\begin{proposition}[Reserved Return under the Two Phase Interactions]\label{prop:writable-tower-reserved-return}
With memory $m=s/8$ before assignment, the native law has unique successful action $u=-\sigma s/128$ among the two actions $u=\pm1/128$. The interaction rival with sign reversal under either pulse predicts $u=s/128$ when the sign is known. On the native plant its action succeeds at $\sigma=-1$ and fails at $\sigma=+1$. Success is distance at most $1/256$ from phase zero and these decisions are unchanged by terminal phase errors of at most $3^{-10}/4$. A preceding public neutral observation can reveal $s$ while preserving the equality of the current readouts. With memory ablated to zero before reassignment both allowed actions fail.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
At assignment the first bit is zero, so there is no phase displacement or memory load from the pulse. The native post-assignment memory is $\sigma s/16$. The rival's is $-s/16$. The write cannot change the leading bit. With a neutral action the respective predicted phases are $u+\sigma s/128$ and $u-s/128$ modulo one. The two native action losses in phase distance are zero and $1/64$ turns. Their distance from the decision threshold $1/256$ exceeds $3^{-10}/4$. With zero memory both distances are $1/128$, which also stays above threshold at that noise bound. Reversing $\sigma$ reverses the native optimal port and leaves the magnitude and budget fixed.

For the public-history control, start one step earlier at memory $s/4$ and apply the public neutral step with zero input. Its early-phase output is $s/32$ in the short signed representative and the remaining memory is $s/8$. The assignment step erases that phase cue from the current observation. The single prior observation still identifies $s$, and the two interaction laws produce the port predictions above. For calibration use distinct preparations with initial memory $s/16$, a public neutral observation and then assignment. The early cue is $s/128$ and the native post-assignment neutral response at zero input is $\sigma s/512$. Evaluating the calibration ports $\pm1/512$ therefore fixes the sign rule from four $(s,\sigma)$ cases. The reserve uses $\pm1/128$ and contributes no calibration outcomes. The interaction contrast on visible histories compares the two specified laws. Paper VIII quantifies over all policies that receive the same public history and budget.
\end{proof}

