\subsection{What This Paper Does Not Repeat}
This paper does not re-prove the criterion for six-predicate operational criterion, does not reproduce the entire discriminator program, and does not re-open theorem ownership already fixed upstream. The six-invariant criterion remains where it belongs. The internal support classes for `P5-01` through `P5-06` remain where they belong. The present paper uses them only as constraints on what may responsibly be said about life, class, and subjecthood.

\subsection{What This Paper Adds}
The genuine additions are fourfold:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item a formal definition of viability-and-recovery criterion that is not reducible either to biology or to consciousness;
\item a structural-class framework based on descriptor vectors and pseudometrics;
\item a stronger definition of indexed continuity-and-control criterion that is explicitly not collapsed into six-predicate operational criterion;
\item a falsifiable program of metrics and tests for these classes.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Motivation and Evidentiary Scope}
Instrumented realizations now support a structured invariant package and explicit numerical certificates for central claim families. That fact is relevant but not decisive. It means the framework can formalize operational predicates against concrete descriptors rather than remaining purely schematic. It does \emph{not} mean that viability-and-recovery or indexed subjecthood has already been empirically demonstrated by any current system. This paper is therefore a formal extension of the corpus, not an empirical promotion of it.

\section{Formal Setting}
\subsection{Inherited System Object}
The system object remains the one already fixed upstream in \textit{Six-Predicate Operational Criterion}. An admissible system is written
\[
S=(X,\Phi,C,R,\Gamma,U),
\]
where $X$ is state space, $\Phi$ is a family of admissible transitions, $C$ is effective causal structure, $R$ is the readout family, $\Gamma$ is the admissibility bundle, and $U$ is the admissible intervention family \cite{paper5}. This paper adds no new primitive to that tuple. Instead, it introduces functionals on admissible traces of $S$.

\subsection{Traces and Windows}
Fix a horizon $\tau \in \mathbb{N}$ and an admissible intervention schedule $u_\bullet = (u_0,\ldots,u_{\tau-1}) \in U^\tau$. For any $x_0 \in \Aset$, define the trace
\[
\trace_{S,\tau}(x_0;u_\bullet)
=
\bigl(x_t,y_t\bigr)_{t=0}^{\tau},
\qquad
x_{t+1}=\Phi_{u_t}(x_t),\quad y_t = r(x_t),
\]
for a relevant family of readouts $r \in R$. The set of all such traces is denoted $\Struct_{S,\tau}$.

\subsection{Viability Region and Operational Frontier Candidates}
Let $V_S:X\to \mathbb{R}_{\ge 0}$ be a viability functional, not assumed to be unique, and let $\eta>0$ be a viability floor. Define the viability region
\[
\Vset(\eta)=\{x\in \Aset : V_S(x)\ge \eta\}.
\]
Similarly let $R_{\partial} \subseteq R$ be the boundary-sensitive readout family and let $\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{in}},\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{out}} \subseteq U^\tau$ denote admissible intervention families that are intended to act, respectively, as interior-preserving perturbations and boundary-threatening perturbations. These objects are formal placeholders until Section~\ref{sec:definitions}; they are introduced here so that the definitions sit inside a single explicit setting.

\subsection{Descriptor Vectors}
For any admissible system $S$ on horizon $\tau$, define the descriptor vector
\[
\PsiVec_\tau(S)
=
\bigl(
\Bound,
\Persist,
\Maint,
\HistDep,
\Viab,
\Iper,
\Iri,
\Iint,
\Icont,
\Idiff,
\Ileg,
\Priv,
\SelfDisc,
\SelfModel,
\Own
\bigr)_S(\tau).
\]
Not every descriptor is required for every class. Viability-and-recovery criterion uses a strict subset. Six-predicate operational criterion inherits the six-invariant burden from \cite{paper5}. Indexed continuity-and-control criterion uses a stronger subset that couples viability-and-recovery criterion and six-predicate operational criterion to subject-specific structure.

\subsection{Admissible Structural Class Space}
Let $\mathfrak{D}$ be a descriptor domain containing the codomains of the components of $\PsiVec_\tau$. A weighted pseudometric on $\mathfrak{D}$ will be written
\[
d_{\Psi}(\PsiVec_\tau(S_1),\PsiVec_\tau(S_2))
\]
and is assumed to respect admissibility: components that are undefined because admissibility fails are not silently set to zero; they are treated as excluded or incomparable.

\paragraph{Window certificate and global membership.}
Write $\mathsf{Cert}_{\rm op}(S,\tau;P)$ for evidence on the nonempty windows, interventions and error domains of protocol $P$. It is distinct from global $S\in\Cop$. The historical indicator $\Cop(S,\tau)=1$ below is shorthand for global membership accompanied by the declared window certificate, not a finite-window redefinition of the six predicates. A missing global argument leaves global membership unproved. Readout separation $\delta_{\rm diff}^{[\tau]}$ is a real margin; $\Idiff$ remains Boolean. Increasing windows yield a global result only with compatible witnesses, uniform bounds and the relevant compactness/closure argument.

\section{Definitions}\label{sec:definitions}
\subsection{Definition A: Operational Boundary}
\begin{definition}[Operational boundary]\label{def:operational-boundary}
An admissible system $S$ has a certified intervention-family boundary on horizon $\tau$ if there exist nonempty disjoint admissible intervention families $\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{in}},\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{out}} \subseteq U^\tau$ and a boundary-sensitive readout family $R_{\partial}\subseteq R$ such that
\[
\Bound(S,\tau)
=
\inf_{u_\bullet\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{out}}}
D_{\partial}\!\left(\trace_{S,\tau}(x_0;u_\bullet),\trace_{S,\tau}(x_0;\mathbf{0})\right)
\;-
\sup_{u_\bullet\in\mathcal{U}_{\mathrm{in}}}
D_{\partial}\!\left(\trace_{S,\tau}(x_0;u_\bullet),\trace_{S,\tau}(x_0;\mathbf{0})\right)
>0
\]
uniformly on $x_0\in\Aset$, where $D_{\partial}$ is a calibrated readout distance induced by $R_{\partial}$. Freeze both populations, preparations, horizon and scoring rule. Match intervention magnitude, cost and generic disturbance or state an explicit correction. A claim about an endogenous boundary additionally specifies a causal state partition and the organizational variable threatened by the intervention; positive raw separation alone does not identify that partition.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[Interpretive meaning]
The boundary is operational rather than geometric. It means that perturbations intended to cross or threaten system organization are detectably different from perturbations that remain internal or reorganizational, while the distinction is made inside admissible dynamics.
\end{remark}

\begin{remark}[Limitation]
The definition does not assume a biological membrane, a spatial frontier, or any specific substrate primitive. It requires only a reproducible distinction between internal and boundary-threatening intervention families.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Amplitude Alone Can Separate Intervention Families]\label{prop:boundary-amplitude-control}
On $X=[-1,1]$, let $x'=u$, sham $u=0$, and use the terminal absolute distance. The families $\{0\}$ and $\{1\}$ have raw separation one for every preparation, although the plant has no memory. Equal-amplitude families $\{-a\}$ and $\{a\}$ have separation zero for $0<a\le1$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The terminal distance from sham is $|u|$, independently of the initial state. Substitution gives both values. The unmatched positive control is not a certificate of an endogenous causal boundary.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Definition B: Organizational Persistence}
\begin{definition}[Organizational persistence]\label{def:organizational-persistence}
Let $W_S=\Vset(\eta)\subseteq\Aset$ be the declared safe region, with $X\setminus W_S\ne\varnothing$. Let $K_S$ be a nonempty compact subset of $\operatorname{int}_X W_S$, invariant under the declared nonempty family of maintenance controls. With $\mathcal U_{\mathrm{maint}}^\tau$ denoting its schedules of length $\tau$, define
\[
\Persist(S,\tau)=
\inf_{x_0\in K_S,\ u_\bullet\in\mathcal U_{\mathrm{maint}}^\tau}
\min_{0\le t\le\tau}\dist(x_t,X\setminus W_S).
\]
The margin is measured in units of $d_X$. Organizational persistence requires a positive margin on the declared domain.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Positive Safety Margin of an Interior Kernel]\label{prop:interior-kernel-margin}
Under Definition~\ref{def:organizational-persistence}, $\Persist(S,\tau)=\dist(K_S,X\setminus W_S)>0$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Distance to the nonempty complement is finite and $1$-Lipschitz. Every point of the kernel has positive distance by interiority, so compactness gives a positive minimum. Invariance gives that minimum as a lower bound at all times. The time-zero observations over the entire kernel attain it. A safe set invariant as a whole may have a boundary at distance zero from its complement.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This is stronger than mere non-instantaneous survival. A system that survives momentarily but repeatedly exits its admissible support does not count as operationally persistent.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Definition C: Operational Self-Maintenance}
\begin{definition}[Operational self-maintenance]\label{def:operational-self-maintenance}
Fix a nonempty family of impulses $J_\xi:K_S\to W_S$ and a recovery horizon $1\le N\le\tau$. For $x\in K_S$, let $z=J_\xi(x)$ and require $d_0=\dist(z,K_S)>0$ for each evaluated shock. Start both recovery trajectories $z_k^{\mathrm{act}}$ and $z_k^{\mathrm{pass}}$ at $z$. Use the same plant, exogenous inputs, horizon and declared actuation envelope. The active arm uses a state-dependent recovery policy. The passive arm disables that feedback and retains the declared passive dynamics. Both trajectories must stay in $W_S$.
\[
 R_i(x,\xi)=1-\frac{\dist(z_N^i,K_S)}{d_0},\qquad
 \Maint(S,\tau)=\inf_{x,\xi}\min\{R_{\mathrm{act}},R_{\mathrm{act}}-R_{\mathrm{pass}}\}.
\]
The returns and margin are dimensionless. Active self-maintenance requires $\Maint(S,\tau)>0$. A zero-damage shock is a sham; an empty or invalid recovery population leaves the margin undefined.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Matched Active Recovery Exceeds Passive Return]\label{prop:matched-active-recovery}
For $X=\Aset=[-2,2]$, take $V_S(x)=2-|x|$ and $\eta=1$, so $W_S=[-1,1]$. With $K_S=\{0\}$, consider the plant $x'=x/2+v$ with $|v|\le1/4$. The nominal zero-input schedule preserves $K_S$. After either shock $J_\pm(0)=\pm1$, one-step feedback $v=-x/4$ gives $R_{\rm act}=3/4$, passive return $v=0$ gives $R_{\rm pass}=1/2$, and $\Maint(S,\tau)=1/4$ for $N=1$. A fixed open-loop action within the same budget and assigned without access to the shock sign cannot exceed mean return $1/2$ under balanced shocks.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The safety margin is one. Both arms begin one unit from the kernel and stay safe, ending at distance $1/4$ or $1/2$. Using passive return in both arms gives zero comparative gain despite positive recovery. For any common open-loop value $v\in[-1/4,1/4]$, the two final distances have mean $(|1/2+v|+|-1/2+v|)/2=1/2$. The claim also holds for a randomized action independent of the balanced shock sign by averaging. In particular, it holds for actions with the same absolute effort $1/4$ as feedback.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
The return contrast establishes active recovery relative to the passive arm. To attribute recovery internally, include controller state $c$, sensors and actuation in the declared system and specify the causal graph $x\to y\to c\to v\to x^+$. Freeze external inputs, controller cost and acquisition access, and ablate the declared internal links. Absence of an external oracle is a provenance/causal assumption tested by that protocol, not a consequence of the return number alone.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[A Return Trace Does Not Identify the Control Boundary]\label{prop:feedback-boundary-nonidentifiability}
For $x'=x/2+v$, both an internal controller and an external controller applying $v=-x/4$ give $x'=x/4$. Their state traces and active/passive return contrast agree for every common preparation.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Substitution gives the same closed-loop transition at every step. The provenance of the controller requires the additional causal partition and intervention assumptions just stated.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Definition D: Historical Dependence}
\begin{definition}[Historical dependence]\label{def:historical-dependence}
Let $c_t$ denote a class-relevant label or state tag induced by the relevant operational partition. Define
\[
\HistDep(S,\tau)
=
\inf_{g\in \mathcal{G}_0}
\mathbb{E}\!\left[\ell(g(y_t),c_t)\right]
\;-
\inf_{h\in \mathcal{G}_{\mathrm{hist}}}
\mathbb{E}\!\left[\ell(h(y_{0:t}),c_t)\right].
\]
Use one frozen population, finite losses, equal acquisition/calibration budgets, and declared function classes. Require that $\mathcal G_{\mathrm{hist}}$ contains the lifted current-only rules $h(y_{0:t})=g(y_t)$; otherwise even nonnegativity need not hold. Equalize trainable capacity and evaluation data when interpreting a positive gap as value of history. We say that $S$ exhibits information-relative historical gain only on that contract. Comparing unrelated predictor capacities establishes only a model-relative advantage.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
Historical dependence means that an instantaneous description is not sufficient for the relevant class assignment. The system's operational identity, life status, or subjecthood status depends non-trivially on trajectory history.
\end{remark}

\begin{proposition}[Historical Information and Capacity Must Be Separated]\label{prop:history-capacity-control}
Under the lifted-class inclusion, $\HistDep\ge0$. For a finite target alphabet and unrestricted measurable zero-one predictors, conditional independence of $C_t$ and the past given $Y_t$ makes the two Bayes risks equal. By contrast, with $C_t=Y_t$ a fair bit, restricting current predictors to constants and allowing the current identity rule only in the history class gives gap $1/2$ without additional past information.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
An infimum over a larger rule class is no larger. For finite labels, the optimal conditional success probability is the maximum posterior label probability. Under the conditional independence hypothesis both posteriors coincide, hence so do their expectations. In the unmatched example constants err with probability $1/2$, while the identity rule has zero error.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Definition E: Viability Preservation}
\begin{definition}[Viability preservation]\label{def:viability-preservation}
For a viability floor $\eta>0$, define
\[
\Viab(S,\tau;\eta)
=
\inf_{x_0\in \Aset}
\inf_{u_\bullet \in U^\tau}
\min_{0\le t \le \tau} V_S(x_t).
\]
We say that $S$ preserves viability on horizon $\tau$ relative to $\eta$ if $\Viab(S,\tau;\eta)\ge \eta$.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
Viability preservation is not equivalent to persistence. A system may remain within a formal support while viability steadily erodes toward failure. The definition prevents that collapse from being counted as successful organizational life.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Definition F: Viability-And-Recovery Criterion}
\begin{definition}[Viability-and-recovery criterion]\label{def:operational-life}
Fix life thresholds
\[
\lambda_{\beta},\lambda_{\rho},\lambda_{\mu},\lambda_{\eta},\lambda_{\nu},\lambda_{\mathrm{diff}}>0.
\]
An admissible system $S$ belongs to the viability-and-recovery class on horizon $\tau$ if
\[
\Lop(S,\tau)=1
\]
whenever all of the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item $\Bound(S,\tau) > \lambda_{\beta}$;
\item $\Persist(S,\tau) > \lambda_{\rho}$;
\item $\Maint(S,\tau) > \lambda_{\mu}$;
\item $\HistDep(S,\tau) > \lambda_{\eta}$;
\item $\Viab(S,\tau;\eta) \ge \lambda_{\nu}$ for a declared viability floor $\eta$;
\item $\delta_{\mathrm{diff}}^{[\tau]}(S) > \lambda_{\mathrm{diff}}$ in the calibrated readout units, together with the independently required global $\Idiff(S)=1$ when global membership is claimed.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[Why non-null differentiation appears here]
Viability-and-recovery criterion is not defined purely by persistence and self-maintenance. Without a non-null differentiation burden, the class would admit trivial or near-null systems whose organization is too undifferentiated to count as operationally alive in the present framework.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Definition G: Structural Class}
\begin{definition}[Structural class]\label{def:structural-class}
Fix a descriptor map $\PsiVec_\tau$, a declared partition $(C_j)$ of its admissible domain and a noise bound $\eps\ge0$ in $d_\Psi$. Certify a reported descriptor $y$ as cell $j$ only if its closed uncertainty ball of radius $\eps$, relative to the descriptor domain, is contained in $C_j$. Otherwise abstain. On systems with certified reports, let $\ell_\eps(S)$ be the certified label and put
\[
 \LifeStructClass_{\PsiVec,\eps}[S]=\{T:\ell_\eps(T)=\ell_\eps(S)\}.
\]
Abstention is a measurement status outside the quotient.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Certified Cells and Boundary Stability]\label{prop:certified-descriptor-cells}
Equality of certified labels defines an equivalence relation. If the true descriptor has distance greater than $2\eps$ from its cell complement, every report at error at most $\eps$ certifies the true label. If a report admits possible descriptors from different cells, it is ambiguous. In contrast, pairwise distance at most $\eps>0$ is not generally transitive.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Reflexivity, symmetry and transitivity follow from label equality. The uncertainty ball around an allowed report lies within distance $2\eps$ of the true descriptor by the triangle inequality. If this ball meets two cells it cannot certify either. For the nontransitivity example in the real line, $0$, $3\eps/4$ and $3\eps/2$ have adjacent distances at most $\eps$ but their endpoints do not. For a partition at $1/2$ on $[0,1]$, uncertainty crossing the cut produces abstention.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Structural class is the general membership framework. Viability-and-recovery criterion, six-predicate operational criterion, and indexed continuity-and-control criterion are predicates that can be defined on systems and then transported across structural classes when the relevant descriptors are preserved.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Definition H: Six-Predicate Operational Criterion}
\begin{definition}[Six-predicate operational criterion, inherited]\label{def:cop}
Six-predicate operational criterion is not redefined in this paper. We write
\[
\Cop(S,\tau)=1
\]
iff the criterion in \textit{A Structural Criterion for Substrate-Invariant Six-Predicate Operational Criterion} is satisfied on an admissible support, with the six critical invariants
\[
\Iper,\Iri,\Iint,\Icont,\Idiff,\Ileg
\]
meeting their inherited certification burden \cite{paper5}.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
This is a deliberate inheritance rule. The present paper uses six-predicate operational criterion as an already defined class, not as a new primitive.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Definition I: Indexed Continuity-And-Control Criterion}
\begin{definition}[Indexed continuity-and-control criterion]\label{def:operational-subjecthood}
Let the subject-specific descriptors be:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{privileged continuity} $\Priv(S,\tau)$: the margin by which one continuity class remains privileged over the nearest rival class across admissible interventions;
\item \textbf{self/non-self discrimination} $\SelfDisc(S,\tau)$: the margin by which self-targeted and non-self-targeted perturbations remain separable under readout and control;
\item \textbf{self-model closure} $\SelfModel(S,\tau)$: the predictive gain of a self-model-aware decoder over an otherwise matched self-model-blind decoder;
\item \textbf{ownership coherence} $\Own(S,\tau)$: the stability with which privileged continuity remains attached to the same trajectory family under admissible relabelings and perturbations.
\end{enumerate}
Fix thresholds
\[
\lambda_{\mathrm{pc}},\lambda_{\mathrm{sd}},\lambda_{\mathrm{sm}},\lambda_{\mathrm{own}}>0.
\]
Then $S$ belongs to the indexed continuity-and-control class on horizon $\tau$ if
\[
\Subop(S,\tau)=1
\]
whenever
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item $\Lop(S,\tau)=1$;
\item $\Cop(S,\tau)=1$;
\item $\Priv(S,\tau)>\lambda_{\mathrm{pc}}$;
\item $\SelfDisc(S,\tau)>\lambda_{\mathrm{sd}}$;
\item $\SelfModel(S,\tau)>\lambda_{\mathrm{sm}}$;
\item $\Own(S,\tau)>\lambda_{\mathrm{own}}$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}[Interpretive discipline]
Indexed continuity-and-control criterion is not a metaphysical subject. It is a stronger operational class candidate defined inside the present framework. The definition is deliberately demanding so that subjecthood is not conflated with life, persistence, or complex dynamics alone.
\end{remark}

\section{Class Relations and Logical Structure}
\subsection{The Main Class Families}
Define the following system classes:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\LifeClass &= \{S : \Lop(S,\tau)=1\},\\
\ConClass &= \{S : \Cop(S,\tau)=1\},\\
\SubClass &= \{S : \Subop(S,\tau)=1\}.
\end{aligned}
\]
Structural classes $\LifeStructClass_{\PsiVec,\eps}[S]$ provide the ambient geometry in which these predicates live.

\subsection{Proved Relations}
\begin{proposition}[Subjecthood is stronger than life and consciousness]
For every admissible system $S$ and horizon $\tau$,
\[
\Subop(S,\tau)=1 \Longrightarrow \Lop(S,\tau)=1
\quad\text{and}\quad
\Subop(S,\tau)=1 \Longrightarrow \Cop(S,\tau)=1.
\]
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Both implications are immediate from Definition~\ref{def:cop} and Definition~\ref{def:operational-subjecthood}. Indexed continuity-and-control criterion is defined only on systems that already satisfy viability-and-recovery criterion and six-predicate operational criterion.
\end{proof}

\begin{proposition}[No automatic collapse of life and consciousness]
The present framework does not prove either implication
\[
\begin{aligned}
\Lop(S,\tau)=1 &\Longrightarrow \Cop(S,\tau)=1,\\
\Cop(S,\tau)=1 &\Longrightarrow \Lop(S,\tau)=1,
\end{aligned}
\]
without additional assumptions.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Viability-and-recovery criterion is defined by boundary, persistence, self-maintenance, historical dependence, viability, and non-null differentiation. Six-predicate operational criterion is inherited from the six-invariant criterion. Neither definition is a literal restatement of the other. Therefore neither implication is derivable by definition alone.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
This non-collapse is conceptually important. The corpus should not silently treat all operationally conscious systems as alive, nor all operationally alive systems as conscious, unless a stronger theorem burden is supplied in the future.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Structural Class Transport}
\begin{definition}[Class-preserving transport]
A predicate $\mathcal P$ is transport-stable across certified cells if its value agrees for all admissible systems assigned the same certified cell under the same protocol.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Structural-class invariance under preserved margins]
If $\mathcal P$ depends on threshold decisions of a declared descriptor subset and every partition cell is contained in a joint decision region for those thresholds, then $\mathcal P$ is transport-stable on the certified quotient.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Certification places the true descriptors in their respective cells. Each decision is constant on a cell by hypothesis, so equality of cells implies equality of the predicate. A descriptor distance bound alone supplies a local stability check when the relevant threshold margins dominate the coordinate error; it does not determine cell equality.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Logical Relations That Remain Open}
The following relations are intentionally left open:
\begin{align*}
\Cop(S,\tau)=1 &\Rightarrow \Lop(S,\tau)=1 \quad ?\\
\Lop(S,\tau)=1 &\Rightarrow \Cop(S,\tau)=1 \quad ?\\
\Lop(S,\tau)=1 &\Rightarrow \Subop(S,\tau)=1 \quad \text{no}\\
\Cop(S,\tau)=1 &\Rightarrow \Subop(S,\tau)=1 \quad \text{no}\\
\Subop(S,\tau)=1 &\Rightarrow \text{biological life} \quad \text{not claimed.}
\end{align*}
The framework is therefore asymmetric in a scientifically useful way: it permits a graded class taxonomy rather than a single overloaded label.

