\subsection{Complexity, Connectivity, and Rich Dynamics}
The most persistent misclassification pressure comes from scale variables. High complexity, dense connectivity, rich temporal variation, or activity richness can produce surfaces that look life-like, consciousness-like, or subject-like under a weak diagnostic language. The present paper explicitly rejects that move. Complexity is at most permissive background. It is never constitutive by itself.

\subsection{Proxy Contamination}
Proxy contamination deserves explicit treatment because the runtime and release-facing layers are not identical. Some descriptors are closer to direct runtime signals, while others inherit part of their evidentiary burden from diagnostic layers, admissibility decisions, or downstream projections. This does not invalidate the class program, but it imposes a discipline: a class should be treated as stronger when its defining coordinates are driven by direct or mostly-native runtime semantics, and weaker when proxy weight dominates.

\subsection{Boundary Ambiguity}
Boundary ambiguity is not a nuisance artifact; it is an expected structural region. The mistake is to treat it either as confirmation or as disconfirmation without localization. A good class system should make boundary ambiguity visible, local, and diagnostically interpretable. That is why near-miss definitions, confounder tracking, and explicit boundary regions are part of the paper rather than afterthoughts.

\section{Formal Closure Strengthening}
This paper does not attempt theorem inflation, but it can still state more precisely what is and is not formally closed.

\begin{criterion}[Class-readiness criterion]
A class predicate is \emph{formally ready} only if:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item its constitutive coordinates are explicit;
\item its boundary geometry is explicit;
\item its negative controls are identifiable;
\item its admissible falsifiers are stated.
\end{enumerate}
Viability-and-recovery criterion, structural class, and indexed continuity-and-control criterion satisfy this criterion within the limits declared by the paper.
\end{criterion}

\begin{criterion}[Subjecthood non-collapse criterion]
No system should be admitted into indexed continuity-and-control criterion unless the following two separations remain open and empirically testable:
\[
\Lop(S,\tau)=1 \not\Rightarrow \Subop(S,\tau)=1,
\qquad
\Cop(S,\tau)=1 \not\Rightarrow \Subop(S,\tau)=1.
\]
If either separation becomes definitionally impossible, subjecthood collapses into a weaker class and loses its intended meaning.
\end{criterion}

\begin{remark}[What is closed]
The paper closes the classificatory language, the class geometry, the principal non-equivalences, and the test grammar. It does not close empirical instantiation, external validation, or metaphysical interpretation.
\end{remark}

\subsection{Phase Repair on the Common Circle-Prefix Tower}

Take the seven-profile support of Paper V \cite{paper5} and Model M of the observable feedback extension in Paper VI \cite{paper6}. Set $\lambda=1/12$, $a=1/2$, and $\kappa=1/8$. Write the ambient space as $X=\mathcal P\times\mathbb T\times B\times[-1/3,1/3]$, where $B=\{0,1\}^{\mathbb N_{>0}}$. The transition preserves $p$, sends $b$ to $Rb=(0,1-b_1,1-b_2,\ldots)$, and updates phase and memory by
\[
 \theta'=u+q\lambda b_1+\kappa\mathbf 1_{\{q=0\}}m \pmod 1,
 \qquad
 m'=a s(q)m+\lambda q b_1 .
\]
Here $q\in\{-1,0,1\}$ and $s(q)=-1$ only at $q=-1$. Use the four observed channels $r_j=\theta+p_j$ for $0\le j\le 2$ and the ternary code $c(b)$. The state distance within a profile is $\max\{\rho(\theta,\tilde\theta),|c(b)-c(\tilde b)|,|m-\tilde m|/4\}$, with circular distance $\rho$ in turns. Across profiles the distance is one.

\begin{proposition}[Viability and Active Phase Repair on the Common Tower]\label{prop:common-tower-phase-repair}
Set $V_S=1/3-|m|$ and $\eta=1/12$, with $A_S=W_S=\{|m|\le 1/4\}$ in $X$. Let $b^\star=(0,1,0,1,\ldots)$ and put
\[
 K_S=\mathcal P\times\{0\}\times\{b^\star\}\times[-1/16,1/16].
\]
The safe region is invariant under all tower commands and $\Viab(S,\tau;\eta)=1/12$. The compact kernel is invariant under the maintenance controls $u=0$, $q=\pm1$, with $\Persist(S,\tau)=3/64$. Under the matched impulses and phase budget below, at recovery horizon $N=1$,
\[
 R_{\rm act}=1,\qquad R_{\rm pass}=1/2,\qquad \Maint(S,\tau)=1/2
 \qquad(\tau\ge 1).
\]
With absolute memory measurement error at most $1/32$, the same policy guarantees $\Maint(S,\tau)\ge3/8$. On the restricted support the original identity and split-readout integration bounds of Papers V--VI still hold in their declared sectors.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
On $W_S$, $|m'|\le 5/24<1/4$. The viability infimum is attained at time zero on the boundary of the memory band. In $K_S$ the leading bit is zero and $Rb^\star=b^\star$. The maintenance controls keep phase zero and contract the magnitude of memory. The nearest approach to $X\setminus W_S$ holds the other coordinates fixed and increases $|m|$ from $1/16$ to just above $1/4$. Thus Proposition~\ref{prop:interior-kernel-margin} gives $(1/4-1/16)/4=3/64$.

For $\sigma\in\{-1,1\}$ use $J_\sigma:K_S\to W_S$ with
\[
 J_\sigma(p,0,b^\star,m)=(p,\sigma/32,b^\star,\sigma/8).
\]
Both arms start at the same shocked state, a distance $d_0=1/32$ from $K_S$. Both use $q=0$ and zero exogenous phase input with the same available adjustment $|u|\le1/64$. The active arm sets $u$ to the projection of $-\kappa\widehat m$ onto this interval using its internal measurement $\widehat m$. The passive arm sets $u=0$. In both arms the terminal memory is $\sigma/16$. With exact measurement the active phase is zero and the passive phase is $\sigma/64$. The terminal distances to the kernel are therefore zero and $1/64$. Projection onto the action interval is $1$-Lipschitz and preserves the exact action. Hence memory error at most $1/32$ gives phase residual at most $1/256$, active return at least $7/8$, and gain at least $3/8$. Both arms stay in $W_S$.

The memory restriction retains every $(p,\theta,b)$, including the copy at $m=0$. Thus the projected support and its profile separation $g=1/80$ agree with Paper V. The lower split-readout bound uses initial observations and the two nonzero signs, whose phase transitions are independent of $m$. The same factor-fibre argument applies on $W_S$, giving lower bound $1/216$ and the exact-initial-readout lower bound $\lambda/2=1/24$. The comparator assigning the phase channels to one factor can use $u+q\lambda/2$ at every step. The error is at most $\lambda/2$ for nonzero $q$ and at most $1/32$ for $q=0$, with the bit update exact. This proves the same upper bound and exact-initial-readout infimum. The common-command contraction bound of Paper VI also restricts to $W_S$.
\end{proof}

For balanced shocks, any open-loop adjustment $v\in[-1/64,1/64]$ assigned independently of the shock sign leaves mean terminal distance
\[
 \tfrac12\bigl(|v+1/64|+|v-1/64|\bigr)=1/64 .
\]
Its mean return is $1/2$, including actions at the same absolute effort as exact feedback. Disabling feedback in both arms gives zero gain. The shock changes phase and memory, and neutral dynamics couple memory to the next phase. Feedback corrects that phase contribution while both arms retain the same passive memory contraction.

The viability statement uses the invariant admissible band. Requiring this same floor throughout the ambient $X$ would fail at time zero where $|m|=1/3$. The result supplies viability, persistence, and maintenance on one model. The operational boundary, class-relevant historical dependence, and the inherited joint certificate remain separate obligations for class membership.

\subsection{Boundary and Historical Repair in the Writable Tower}

\begin{proposition}[Safe Prefix Perturbations and Historical Repair on the Common Tower]\label{prop:writable-tower-viability}
On the writable common support of Paper V \cite{paper5}, Proposition~\ref{prop:common-tower-phase-repair} retains $\Viab=1/12$, $\Persist=3/64$, and $\Maint\ge3/8$ at its stated memory error bound. The following matched protocols also give an operational prefix boundary margin $\Bound=2/27$ and a historical repair-action margin $\HistDep=1/2$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The memory law is unchanged for all writes and probes, so $W_S$ and its viability floor remain valid on all admissible trajectories. The native maintenance and recovery commands, kernel and metric remain the same. The comparison at equal budget therefore retains exact returns $1$ and $1/2$, and the robust lower gain $3/8$.

For the boundary protocol use seven common neutral native steps, followed by one terminal step. Define the interior action as a phase input $1/8$ with no write. Define the boundary-threatening action as a write opposite to $b^\star_3$ at site three, with phase input zero and no probe. Compare each action with the zero-input sham and use terminal code distance. The common neutral steps insert a reference prefix for every initial tape. The interior action has code distance zero, and the write gives distance $2/27$ for every initial state in $W_S$. This tests disruption of the organized reference prefix inside the viable band. That prefix controls the role probes when the corresponding slot is active.

For history, use the balanced initial memories $\pm1/8$, with the same profile, phase zero and tape $b^\star$. A neutral zero-input step followed by a positive native pulse yields phase zero and memory $\pm1/32$ at the second observation. The four current readouts are identical across the two arms. Define the repair class by the unique action in $\{-1/256,1/256\}$ that returns the phase to zero at the next neutral step. This is a repair-action tag of the current state. The wrong action gives phase distance $1/128$. The first post-preparation phase, $\pm1/64$, determines the correct tag. The best snapshot and history predictors have zero-one risk $1/2$ and $0$, respectively. A snapshot predictor given the past phase ties, and using the same passive action in both recovery arms gives zero comparative gain.
\end{proof}

The margins concern a prefix perturbation and a particular recovery decision. They give positive threshold choices for the life descriptors in Definition~\ref{def:operational-life} on the declared support and protocols, together with the inherited non-null witness. The additional subjecthood descriptors of Definition~\ref{def:operational-subjecthood} require their own evidence.

\subsection{Causal Lineage Tests on the Common Tower}

The following comparison uses the writable plant of Paper V \cite{paper5}, run in matched replicas. Let $\eta=3^{-10}/4$ in the four-channel weighted maximum norm with phase weights $(1,1/3,1/9)$ and code weight one. The sign and loss calculations use the early phase $r_0=\theta$, measured in turns. Use the short signed representative about zero and abstain for sign readings of magnitude at most $\eta$. Start on a common profile $p$ in the kernel of Proposition~\ref{prop:common-tower-phase-repair}. The typed preparation impulse
\[
 H_s(p,0,b^\star,m)=(p,0,b^\star,s/8),\qquad s\in\{-1,1\},
\]
lies in $W_S$. It changes memory alone and is used as an initial preparation, rather than a new tower command. When $s$ is hidden, the public history, the state readout at the impulse and the subsequent choice set are identical before the first neutral response. The physical slots of the paired replicas and the intervention that prepared each slot fix its lineage. This reference is recorded independently of the subsequent phase responses. Trial populations balance the two signs and keep the profile and other preparation coordinates matched.

\begin{proposition}[Four Finite Operational Subject Descriptors on the Common Tower]\label{prop:writable-tower-subject-descriptors}
On the stated matched trial populations, privileged continuity against a readout clone has clean gap at least $1/256$ turns over four successor observations. The equal-cost discrimination contrast has absolute clean gap $1/64$ turns and reverses under causal reassignment. The internal-memory return predictor beats the public-history predictor by at least $1/2$ in zero-one action loss. The ownership agreement score is one under retention and simultaneous relabeling and zero under the causal transplant. These conclusions hold under the stated readout noise, with phase-distance margins reduced by at most $2\eta$ and with internal memory error at most $1/32$ in the prediction trial.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
\emph{Privileged continuity.} For a task reference $s$ fixed before the response, compare $H_s$ with its readout clone $H_{-s}$. They have the same time-zero readout. Apply the same schedule $q=0$, $u_t=-s2^{-t}/64$, without writes or probes, to both replicas for $t=0,1,2,3$. Before step $t+1$ their memories are $s2^{-t}/8$ and $-s2^{-t}/8$. Their next phases are zero and $-s2^{-t}/32$. The line that continues the kernel phase under the common challenge is selected by the smaller observed distance to zero. Its worst-case clearance over the competing line is at least $1/256-2\eta>0$. An exact state clone follows the same phase path under the same commands and gives no privileged margin. The claim concerns the selected task reference in this comparison, with no uniqueness claim under cloning of the full state.

\emph{Self/non-self discrimination.} Start at $H_s$ and apply $q=\sigma\in\{-1,1\}$, $u=0$. The first bit is zero, so the two signs of $q$ give identical immediate public readouts and memories $\sigma s/16$. In two fresh copies of this prepared state, apply respectively the two action ports $u=\pm1/128$, $q=0$. The cost $|u|$ is identical, as is the assignment step cost $|q|$. Write $L_\pm$ for the resulting circular distance to zero under the positive and negative ports. Then $L_+-L_-=\sigma s/64$. Thus the successful port follows the causal assignment and reverses when $\sigma$ changes, even though the port names and costs stay fixed. With memory zero both losses are $1/128$ and there is no clean contrast. This test uses phase return as the outcome rather than a port label as evidence.

\emph{Self-model closure.} At the hidden preparation $H_s$, score selection between $u=\pm1/64$, $q=0$, by whether the selected action minimizes the next circular distance to zero. The right action gives zero and the other gives $1/32$. Both predictors receive the same public history, with common readout errors across the matched sign preparations. The model-aware predictor additionally has the internal estimate $\widehat m$ and forecasts each outcome with $u+\kappa\widehat m$. Its sign and therefore its chosen port are correct for $|\widehat m-m|\le1/32$. A predictor using only the public history cannot distinguish the balanced signs, even with randomization independent of the preparation. Its expected action error is $1/2$ and its expected true phase loss is $1/64$. The prediction trial measures the added information about return, with an informed rival given the same internal estimate tying. The observed winning action remains identifiable because $1/32>2\eta$.

\emph{Ownership coherence.} Retain the recorded assignment of the two signs to the two physical slots. Apply a common hidden assignment command $q=\sigma$, $u=0$, to both slots. An optional common write at one of the writable sites during that step preserves the leading zero. The measured phase and code after assignment are independent of $\sigma$. The actual two memories after $q=-1$ are the swapped two memories after $q=1$, while the other state coordinates in corresponding slots agree. Under a subsequent neutral diagnostic with $u=0$, the phases are $\sigma s/128$. This sign decoder uses the physical readout and no lineage record. Compare its signs with the fixed preparation record and report the fraction of slots in agreement. Retention gives one, and the causal swap gives zero. A permutation of the names of the slots acts on the recorded and decoded vectors together and leaves the agreement invariant. Zero memory yields an abstention under every allowed noise error, while a nonzero response remains sign-decodable since $1/128>2\eta$. Thus the comparison distinguishes renaming, harmless perturbation and transfer of a causal state within the declared experiment.
\end{proof}

