\section{Residual Runtime and Interpretation Limits}
This paper remains intentionally restrictive at the empirical and interpretive boundary.

\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{No automatic instance certification.} Concrete system realizations and diagnostics motivate the classificatory layer, but they do not by themselves certify any concrete instance as belonging to the viability-and-recovery or indexed continuity-and-control classes.
\item \textbf{No external-validation claim.} Internal structural hardening, admissibility discipline, clean-environment reproduction, and refined diagnostics do not amount to independent external validation.
\item \textbf{No total closure of the philosophical problem.} The paper proposes a falsifiable operational program. It does not claim to resolve every philosophical question about life, consciousness, or subjectivity.
\item \textbf{No automatic promotion from class logic to present-system assignment.} Any concrete assignment remains a future empirical burden governed by admissibility, comparators, and external review.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Discussion}
The main contribution of this paper is classificatory, not foundational. The upstream corpus already supplied the burdens required for rigid identity, operational continuity, non-nullity, admissibility, and a six-predicate operational criterion. What remained absent was a disciplined way to speak about higher-order classes without sliding either into biology-by-default or into metaphysical inflation. The paper addresses that absence by introducing three linked but non-identical layers.

First, the viability-and-recovery criterion defines a class of systems that preserve organizational viability across time and perturbation while maintaining a genuine operational frontier and non-null differentiation. This is a stronger concept than persistence and a weaker concept than subjecthood. Second, structural class provides the general descriptor-relative geometry in which class membership and transport can be discussed across admissible realizations. Third, the indexed continuity-and-control criterion provides a further strengthening: it inherits viability-and-recovery and the six-predicate operational criterion, then adds privileged continuity, self/non-self asymmetry, self-model closure, and ownership coherence.

This organization resolves a recurrent ambiguity between informal vocabulary and operational definitions. Terms such as ``alive,'' ``conscious,'' and ``subject'' are often deployed as if they were either interchangeable or disconnected. Here they correspond to distinct operational classes: life criteria do not collapse into consciousness criteria, consciousness criteria do not collapse into subjecthood criteria, and subjecthood cannot be reduced to complexity, persistence, or substrate. Structural class supplies the common coordinate geometry in which these distinctions remain explicit and testable.

The classificatory layer is also bounded by present empirical instrumentation. Instrumented realizations now establish an explicit operational chain from system trajectories to invariant packages, boundary diagnostics, and admissibility ledgers, making a higher-order classification layer formal rather than speculative. Nevertheless, closure burdens at the independent adjudication layer remain open, and no current concrete realization establishes operational life or operational subjecthood.

That is precisely why the present paper is useful. It separates three questions that are otherwise too easily fused:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item what classes are formally definable within the causally rigid framework;
\item what tests would be required to support membership in those classes;
\item what specific present systems, if any, actually satisfy those burdens.
\end{enumerate}
The first question is what this paper answers. The second is what its falsifiable program formalizes. The third remains an empirical burden for future admissible campaigns.

\subsection{Relation to Instrumented Realizations}
Concrete realizations do not serve here as proof of viability-and-recovery or subjecthood. Rather, they demonstrate that the framework carries enough operational structure to define explicit class predicates without hand-waving. In particular, instrumented models yield concrete descriptors for persistence, rigid identity, integration, continuity, differentiation, and legibility, making it possible to formulate life- and subjecthood-relevant descriptors as testable observables. The conceptual taxonomy is therefore grounded in operational measurement practice without being reducible to any single implementation.

\subsection{What Would Strengthen This Paper Further}
Advancing this classificatory layer depends on sharper empirical separation rather than further taxonomy. Four developments would materially strengthen the empirical program:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item system-native viability diagnostics that cleanly separate organizational self-maintenance from inert persistence;
\item subjecthood-specific perturbation families that localize privileged continuity, self/non-self asymmetry, and ownership coherence without complexity confounders;
\item stronger cross-substrate class-transport evidence under independent or semi-independent evaluation paths;
\item external reproduction or third-party evaluation capable of testing the class predicates without inheriting the entire internal tooling stack.
\end{enumerate}
Until then, the present paper should be read exactly as intended: as a formal and falsifiable extension of the corpus, not as a declaration that the hardest empirical questions have already been settled.

\subsection{Viability and Recovery on the Asymmetric Hilbert Plant}

\begin{proposition}[A Joint Infinite-Plant Life Certificate]
\label{prop:asymmetric-native-life}
Use the asymmetric native plant of Paper V, with its intrinsic complete X,
global support A and full six-predicate certificate. Fix $\tau=2$ and
\[
 \begin{gathered}
 V_S(x)=1-\max\{|z_1^L|,|z_1^R|\},\quad \eta=1/2,\quad W_S=A,
 \\
 K_S=\{x\in A:\theta=0,\ z_1^i=\bar z_1^i(\sigma),\ i=L,R\}.
 \end{gathered}
\]
The following single plant and declared experimental strata give
\[
 (\Bound,\Persist,\Maint,\HistDep,\Viab)
   =(1/128,45/94,17/64,1/2,1/2),
 \qquad \delta_{\rm diff}^{[2]}\ge1/3,
 \qquad \Idiff=1.
\]
Thus the life criterion holds, for example at thresholds
$1/256,1/4,1/8,1/4,1/4,1/6$ in this order. The fifth is the viability
threshold and the sixth the differentiation threshold.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The set X differs from A only in the larger first coordinate interval of each port.
The safe set is exactly A, its complement in X is nonempty, and K is compact
and contained in its relative interior. The distance from K to that complement
is $1/2-1/47=45/94$ in the unscaled intrinsic state metric. The other target
is $1/49$, and changing index branches costs at least one in that metric.
This is not
interiority of an infinite cube in the unrestricted Hilbert ambient space.

The maintenance command uses $q=u=0$, native clipped feedback on both first
coordinates with their targets $\bar z_1^i(\sigma)$, and zero other inputs.
On K the phase is zero and both inputs vanish. Clipping is inactive, phase
remains zero, and both coordinates retain their targets; hence K is invariant.
Both shocks change only the indexed coordinate by $\pm1/8$, leaving the phase
zero. Under the same native feedback the only nonzero input is
$-4r_{\rm self}(\pm1/8)$, of magnitude $17/128<1/2$. The shocked coordinate
returns exactly to its target in one step. The passive arm disables recovery
feedback and uses the genuine zero-input command $v=0$ on the same actuation
envelope; no hidden phase-compensation controller is retained. It ends at error
$\pm r_{\rm self}/8$. Other coordinates, controls and preparations are identical.
Distance to K is exactly the shocked-coordinate error: changing index branches
cannot shorten a displacement of size at most $1/8$. Thus
$R_{\rm act}=1$, $R_{\rm pass}=1-r_{\rm self}$, and the comparative margin is
$r_{\rm self}=17/64$. Both arms stay in A. The sensor--controller--actuator loop is
the native one declared in the plant; an external implementation of the same
policy would have the same return traces, so internal provenance remains part
of the explicit causal partition and its link-ablation control.

For the boundary use $z_1^{\rm self}/16$, selected by the native bit $b_9$
from the fixed c-channel, and
the maximum trace distance over the two-step window. In the first step use
$q=u=0$, and compare sham with a pulse on the indexed first coordinate
(outside family) or on its partner (inside family), both of amplitude $a_1$;
the second step is sham. Both pulses have the same norm, cost and timing.
Uniformly on every initial point of A the indexed
pulse changes the measured coordinate by $a_1/64=1/128$; the partner pulse
changes it by zero. The causal partition and regulated organizational variable
are the indexed homeostatic coordinate and its declared return loop. This does
not infer a biological membrane from a scalar contrast.

For the historical stratum choose a fair sign $s$, prepare $\theta_0=s/8$
and $b_4=(1+s)/2$, and use two sham steps. The relevant reading is the fixed
phase channel $r_0$, and the operational tag is the retained bit $b_4$.
The present reading is zero under both preparations, while the initial phase
identifies s. Current-only and history predictors have the same Boolean output
class, one-bit storage capacity and calibration population; the history class
also contains every lifted current rule. Their minimum zero-one risks are
$1/2$ and zero. Supplying the complete current c-channel reveals the retained
tag and makes both risks zero. This mandatory control bounds the claim to
information-relative historical gain, not non-Markovianity of a complete state.

All native commands preserve A, so the viability infimum is at least $1/2$;
time zero at $|z_1^L|=1/2$ attains it. Opposite head bits retain readout
separation at least $1/3$, and their global non-null argument is that of the
