\begin{proof}
In the continuity-locus stratum fix $b_1=0$, all switches on and $\theta=0$.
Compare the regulated state $z=\bar z$ with the readout clone
$z=\bar z-e_1^{\rm self}/4$, with identical bits and phase. Both lie in A
and have the same initial phase reading; their complete c-readings are not
claimed identical. Use the same native challenge
$q=b_4=1$, $u=-\lambda\ell(\bar z)/4$, $v=0$.
The regulated trajectory has next phase zero and the clone has phase
$-3/2048$. The response-clearance score is distance from the predeclared
kernel phase at this first query. On the specified two-candidate family its
nearest alternative therefore has gap $3/2048$. Pad the second step with sham.
These are operational response classes inside one structural identity object,
not two inverse-limit identities. Admitting an exact full-state clone gives
gap zero; admitting arbitrarily close alternatives without a separation
restriction would also destroy this certified finite-family gap. Neither is
silently ruled out by a claim of absolute uniqueness.

Self/non-self discrimination uses the equal-cost port challenge in the life
proof, with the regulated locus selected by the retained native index. The
signed contrast is $1/128$; interchanging the causal role assignment while
holding the reading convention fixed changes its sign. Transporting both
roles and references preserves its magnitude. Thus the separation is tied to
the declared actuation/reading partition, not to unequal pulse amplitudes.

For self-model closure use the reserved physical indexed-routing task:
independent known signs $\sigma,x,y$, command g, and forecast target
$g\sigma xy$ from the normalized Hilbert port contrast. Fix the same
three-operation language, input acquisition, held-out population and loss
for both models. A self-model-blind constitutive register has at most two
randomized-port ancestors, and the final readout is additive. Any finite
admissible hybrid has the form
$f_{12}(\sigma,x)+f_{13}(\sigma,y)+f_{23}(x,y)$ conditional on independent
latent seeds. Its inner product with $\sigma xy$ is zero. Squared error is
therefore at least one on each nonzero target, and the flat/sham contrast
obeys the same bound. With the basal/intervention/contrast weights and
two flat commands of the protocol in Paper VIII, the exact weak infimum is
$14/15$, attained by the zero forecast. The native indexed circuit pays
three operations at rate $1/16$, fits every target and has loss $3/16$.
No two-operation circuit can couple all three signs and g in its reserved
contrast; its contrast contribution alone is at least $1/3>3/16$.
Thus the properly penalized predictive gain is $179/240$. The full grammar,
including all six generators and their closure, is specified in Paper VIII;
this calculation is independent of assuming any subject-class membership.
An unrestricted same-cost emulator gives zero comparative gain and is a
