\section{Canonical estimator family for the subjectivity coordinates}\label{sec:canonical-subjectivity-estimators}
This appendix gives the estimator family for the seven coordinates. Tildes denote signed raw contrasts before the admissibility and normalization step. A zero denominator, invalid domain or missing reference gives insufficient evidence.

\subsection{Estimator for self-index stability}
Let the candidate set be finite with at least two elements, and let $q$ be a normalized nonnegative weight vector obtained by the frozen estimator. For a passive renaming $\pi$, rerun that estimator on jointly renamed data and references, obtaining $q^{[\pi]}$. Compare it after inverse alignment. With total variation on these finite weights, put
\[
 r_{\rm eq}=\sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{\rm adm}}\operatorname{TV}(\pi^{-1}_*q^{[\pi]},q),
 \qquad \widehat{\SelfIndex}_\tau=(q(z^\star)-\max_{z\ne z^\star}q(z))(1-r_{\rm eq}).
\]
Here $z^\star$ is a maximizer; a tie gives zero gap. Both factors lie in $[0,1]$. Exact equivariance of the estimator implies $r_{\rm eq}=0$; it must be checked, not assumed merely from the word relabeling. A causal role swap or transplant without the corresponding reference transport is a different intervention. Within-run role-fixing subgroups in the constructive examples retain their original protocol.

For the negative control $q=(1,0)$, directly comparing the swapped vector to $q$ gives total variation one and destroys the score despite perfect dominance. Inverse alignment restores the vector. Thus passive renaming must not be confused with active reassignment of roles.

\subsection{Estimator for ownership coherence}
Use a preregistered matched nonempty event set $\mathcal E_\tau$ and an independently assigned reference $o^\star$, fixed without consulting candidate or control assignments. Both arms report scores $o(e),o_{\rm ctl}(e)\in[0,1]$ for the same events. For $v$ equal to either assignment, put
\[
 L(v)=|\mathcal E_\tau|^{-1}\sum_{e\in\mathcal E_\tau}|v(e)-o^\star(e)|.
\]
With valid matching, admissible reference values in $[0,1]$ and the declared minimum coverage, report the signed contrast and normalized score separately:
\[
 O_{\rm raw}=L(o_{\rm ctl})-L(o),\qquad
 \widehat{\OwnField}_\tau=\min(1,\max(0,O_{\rm raw})).
\]
If evidence is insufficient, both outputs are undefined and the status records the reason. A valid zero contrast records a tie. A negative contrast records a loss to the baseline even when the normalized score is zero.

\begin{proposition}[Ownership Gain Is Relative to a Shared Reference]\label{prop:ownership-reference-gain}
On valid evidence, $O_{\rm raw}\in[-1,1]$, is nonincreasing in candidate error for fixed baseline error, and is zero for identical assignments. It is invariant under a common bijective renaming of the events and their assignments and reference.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
Each mean absolute error lies in $[0,1]$. Their difference increases when the candidate's reference error decreases. Identical assignments have identical errors, and a common reindexing merely permutes both sums. Changing causal roles without the corresponding reference transformation changes the comparison and is not a renaming.
\end{proof}

A change in distance from the baseline incurs no separate penalty. The reference must encode the intended task or causal event assignment rather than the candidate's self-report. If each assignment and reference value has error bounded by $e$, the triangle inequality bounds the contrast error by $4e$; use the declared uncertainty and decision threshold when interpreting its sign.

\subsection{Estimator for auto\-bio\-graphical continuity}
Let $\mathcal{T}_\tau=\{\kappa_1,\dots,\kappa_m\}$ be the admissible continuity candidates with a designated self-indexed line $\kappa^\star$. Define
\[
\widetilde{\ContField}_\tau
=
\frac{\mathrm{score}(\kappa^\star)-\max_{\kappa\neq \kappa^\star}\mathrm{score}(\kappa)}
{s_C+\mathrm{score}(\kappa^\star)}.
\]
The score function is nonnegative in the fixed calibration and may combine trajectory compatibility, remap robustness, and fork penalties. A denominator with an arbitrary negative score is not admissible; for example scores $-2,-3$ would reverse a positive gap. Invalid inputs leave the estimator undefined. What matters is not the exact formula but that the estimator is gap-based: continuity must be privileged over nearby rivals.

\subsection{Estimator for perspective gain}
Let $M_{\mathrm{1p}}$ be the best first-person indexed predictive factorization and $M_{\mathrm{3p}}$ the best index-neutral factorization on the same admissible support. Define
\[
\widetilde{\Persp}_\tau
=
\frac{\Loss(M_{\mathrm{3p}},\tau)-\Loss(M_{\mathrm{1p}},\tau)}
{s_P+\Loss(M_{\mathrm{3p}},\tau)}.
\]
This estimator turns perspective into a measurable gain rather than an interpretive flourish.

\subsection{Estimator for valuation asymmetry}
Let $\mathfrak{U}_{\mathrm{self}}$ and $\mathfrak{U}_{\mathrm{nonself}}$ be matched perturbation families differing only in whether the target is self-indexed. Let $R(u)$ denote the resulting weighted response. Then
\[
\widetilde{\ValAsym}_\tau
=
\frac{\mathbb{E}_{u\in\mathfrak{U}_{\mathrm{self}}}[R(u)]-
\mathbb{E}_{u\in\mathfrak{U}_{\mathrm{nonself}}}[R(u)]}
{s_V+\mathbb{E}_{u\in\mathfrak{U}_{\mathrm{self}}}[R(u)]}.
\]
This estimator is high only when self-targeted perturbations matter differentially after structural matching.

\subsection{Estimator for intervention selectivity}
Use the five-coordinate signed drops and preregistered targets of the selective-response rule. On the matched admissible target set $\mathcal I_{\rm tgt}\ne\varnothing$ the scalar is
\[
 \widehat{\IntervProf}_\tau =
 \frac{1}{|\mathcal I_{\rm tgt}|}\sum_{u\in\mathcal I_{\rm tgt}}
 {\bf1}\bigl[\text{target drops and absolute off-target changes meet their thresholds}\bigr].
\]
Record the separate matched-sham rate. The score lies in $[0,1]$ when defined. Replacing either derived coordinate while keeping the five primary measurements and protocol fixed leaves the score unchanged.

\subsection{Estimator for irreducibility}
The irreducibility estimator is already defined in the main text, but two further notes matter. First, the complexity penalty should be fixed before the comparison, not tuned after observing the result. Second, the weak rival family should be documented in the artifact layer so that a later critic can see exactly which alternatives were and were not defeated.

\subsection{Composite gate design}
One may choose stricter or softer versions of the gate:
\[
\GateSubj^{(\min)}=\min_j \widehat{X}_j,\qquad
\GateSubj^{(\mathrm{soft})}=\left(\prod_j \widehat{X}_j\right)^{1/7}.
\]
The present paper prefers the strict minimum as the gate and the geometric mean as the scalar because that pairing best separates class membership from within-class ordering. A future implementation may report both.

\subsection{An Explicit Causal Grammar for a Reserved Indexed Task}

\begin{definition}[Two-port causal grammar and its full admissible closure]
\label{def:indexed-causal-grammar}
For the asymmetric native plant of Paper V, draw independently the retained
index $\sigma=2b_9-1$ and the initial signs $x=2b_2-1$, $y=2b_3-1$.
Every predictor receives all three signs, the command $g\in\{-1,0,1\}$,
the same previous observations and the same fixed calibration, before a new
actuation. Previous registers and exogenous randomization are independent of
these freshly randomized inputs. No future residual or precomputed product
is supplied. Inputs can be extracted by a common prefix decoder; that decoder
does not contain the product of the three signs.
The forecast interface has exactly the four decoded input wires
$(g,\sigma,x,y)$ and the independent past registers. It does not also supply
an uncharged packed real encoding of the freshly drawn signs. The full
current preparation is a known deterministic function of these wires, so
there is no information advantage for either model. Constructing, unpacking
or querying a different joint encoding is charged in the same circuit
language. Giving both models a free joint feature is a different, explicitly
tested representation contract, not a consequence of Shannon information
equivalence. The fixed three physical global channels are unchanged.

Use a common unrolled circuit language: input/constant wires; unary or binary
arithmetic, clipping, branching, copying and register operations; and a final
affine readout with fixed coefficients. A command-dependent coefficient,
implicit dispatch on g, or state-dependent choice of program is a charged
operation, not a free change of terminal weights. Fixed signed permutations
of input/output coordinates are interface conventions applied to both classes;
they preserve cost and ancestry, but do not permit multiplying two input wires
for free. Each nontrivial operation costs one, including a nontrivial
index-dependent routing or conditional branch. Internal affine operations
also count. Constants in the shared interface are $0,1$; additional constants
and gates are explicitly described. Copies of raw input wires to terminals
are part of the common interface, not an oracle. Loops, simulator calls and
macros are expanded over the fixed window, so a loop does not hide a sequence
of interactions. Both classes have three operations and one working register
for the reserved forecast; a common fixed acquisition/calibration wrapper is
identical for every model and the complexity is its conditional circuit cost.
In particular Comp is the static number of nontrivial nodes in that unrolled
forecast circuit, not an expected active-gate count or a weighted average
of component costs. A mixture includes the nodes and routing needed by its
components. The scoring wrapper may evaluate the same fixed forecast on
different trials/commands without charging its description anew per data row.

Tag every internal register by the set of randomized ports on which its value
causally depends. A weak constitutive register has tag size at most two.
The command and independent exogenous randomness are conditioned on, not
counted as randomized ports. Final readout may add functions from the allowed
registers, but may not feed their combined output into a new nonlinear or
stateful coordinate violating the tag bound. This is an explicit causal
restriction, not a theorem inferred from the word ``memory'' or from an
absence of phenomenality.

The six generating families are all monitoring, finite-memory, policy,
narrative-token, broadcast and bookkeeping programs satisfying this signature
and common budget. Policy optimization and encoded narrative/state access
must use the same language; a lookup table or a latent tag has its actual
port ancestry and construction cost. Admit every finite composition, mixture
and latent coupling that preserves this signature and total budget. Mixture
weights/latent seeds may be independent exogenous variables or belong to a
registered component with the same ancestry rule. They cannot be free joint
functions of the three ports. This defines the entire experimental instance
$\WeakClosure=\WeakClosure^{(2,\mathcal L,3)}$, not six selected predictors.
The superscript records causal signature, language and budget; it is not
asserted to equal the unrestricted weak class for every instrumented system.
For every fixed command and
exogenous seed its outputs lie in
\[
 \mathcal V_2=\{f_{12}(\sigma,x)+f_{13}(\sigma,y)+f_{23}(x,y)\}.
\]
Constants and one-port terms are included. Conversely, only those elements
realizable by the stated budget are admitted; no universal approximation
claim for the budget is needed. The stronger model may create the joint
register $\sigma xy$, paying for its operations in the same language.

This signature is a prospective instantiation of the no-new-coordinate clause
of the weak closure. General encoders, policies or workspaces without this
restriction form a larger class. They must not be called excluded by the
six family names alone. In particular a same-cost emulator in that larger
class is an obligatory negative control.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Exact Loss Against the Entire Causal Closure]
\label{lem:indexed-causal-closure-loss}
On the balanced sign cube, let $\chi=\sigma xy$. The native controller applies
to Hilbert coordinate j the inputs
\[
 v_j^L=(a_jxy/4)(1+g\sigma),\qquad
 v_j^R=(a_jxy/4)(1-g\sigma),
\]
with $q=u=0$, initial $z=\bar z$, and $\theta=0$. Its normalized physical
contrast at the next step is
\[
 T_g=\frac8{a_j}\bigl[(z_j^{L\prime}-\bar z_j^L)
                         -(z_j^{R\prime}-\bar z_j^R)\bigr]=g\chi.
\]
At $g=0$ the indexed difference vanishes by equal broadcast; the input command
cost remains charged at the same upstream port. Use squared error, three
operations, and $\mathrm{Comp}/16$. Define the loss as one third of the basal
forecast error ($g=1$), one third of its mean over the ten declared intervention
commands (eight with $|g|=1$, two with $g=0$), and one third of the error in
the reserved contrast $T_1-T_0$, plus that penalty. Then
\[
 \inf_{M\in\WeakClosure}\mathcal L(M)=14/15,\qquad
 \min_{M\in\mathcal F_3}\mathcal L(M)=3/16,
\]
where $\mathcal F_3$ is the common-language class with at most three operations
without the weak tag restriction. Its minimizer computes $g\sigma xy$ with
three products. Thus the strict loss margin is $179/240$ and with $s_R=1$
the normalized irreducibility score is $179/464$.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
The two ports have their own sham fixed points. Subtracting those references
in a q-zero step leaves $v/4$; direct substitution proves the displayed
physical contrast. All these coordinates belong to the single globally
coupled infinite plant, not an additional Boolean transition system.

Every $f\in\mathcal V_2$ is orthogonal to $\chi$ under the balanced cube:
each pair term omits one independent mean-zero sign. Consequently
\[
 \mathbb E(\chi-f)^2=1+\mathbb E f^2\ge1.
\]
The identity holds conditionally on every independent latent seed and for
all finite admissible hybrids; averaging does not reduce the bound. Differences
between command-conditioned forecasts also belong to $\mathcal V_2$.
Thus the three loss terms are bounded below by $1/3$, $4/15$ and $1/3$.
The constant-zero program has no nontrivial gate cost and attains all three,
proving the infimum $14/15$ over the entire declared closure.

The three-product circuit has zero error on every task and penalty $3/16$.
A circuit using at most two unary/binary internal operations has at most
three raw inputs in any internal ancestry. Each contribution to its final
affine readout either omits one randomized sign, or is independent of g.
In the latter case it cancels in the command contrast; in the former case
that contrast remains orthogonal to $\chi$. Hence its reserved contrast
alone has squared error at least one and contributes at least $1/3>3/16$.
Any circuit with three operations has penalty at least $3/16$ and nonnegative
errors. This proves optimality without assuming an optimal fitting rule.
The arithmetic gives $14/15-3/16=179/240$ and
$(179/240)/(1+14/15)=179/464$.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Emulator and representation controls.}
The tag restriction is a structural experimental hypothesis, not a conclusion
about all possible monitors or memory models. If a rival can instantiate the
same three-product circuit at the same information and cost, its loss is
$3/16$ and the stronger model's strict margin is at most zero. Supplying a
free latent tag with $\chi$ also invalidates the independent-preparation
assumption and removes the claimed bound. Under a passive exchange of the
physical ports, flip the retained index and the prefix tail from site 9;
the native shift commutes with this operation, x and y are unchanged, and
both $T_g$ and $\chi$ change sign. Pair ancestry, costs and squared losses
are preserved. A hidden index-dependent decoder cannot be declared a free
renaming; its gates and ancestry must be counted for every competitor.

