\begin{theorem}[Nonempty Proper Indexed Class in the Infinite Model Universe]
\label{thm:native-nonempty-subfp}
Equip the asymmetric plant with the complete protocols above, the full declared
causal closure, equal exponents $1/7$, and thresholds
$\lambda_G=1/8192$, $\lambda_\Phi=1/16$. In the same complete instrumented
model universe as Theorem~\ref{thm:infinite-strict-upstream-separation},
\[
 \varnothing\ne\Subfp(2)\cap\mathfrak M_\infty
 \subsetneq\Cop\cap\mathfrak M_\infty.
\]
The statement is relative to the specified causal grammar, comparison domains,
preparations and intrinsic state metric, not to all unrestricted memory or
policy models or to empirical phenomenality.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Paper V proves all six global predicates on the entire infinite support,
with the unchanged full split-product sector and separated identity alternatives.
Paper VII proves all six life clauses and the four indexed continuity
descriptors on this same plant. Proposition~\ref{prop:native-seven-coordinates}
and the intervention programs give all five primary measurements, profile one
and strict irreducibility against every admissible causal hybrid.
The smallest coordinate is $64/266529>1/8192$. The product is
\[
 \PhiSubj^7=\frac{179}{5568}\frac{64}{266529}>(1/8)^7>(1/16)^7.
\]
Monotonicity of the positive seventh root proves the scalar clause with
$\lambda_\Phi>\lambda_G$. All denominators and evidence domains are defined,
so every clause of Definition~\ref{def:subfp} holds, proving nonemptiness.
Upstream inclusion is Proposition~\ref{prop:upstream-inclusion}; the independent
symmetric infinite-dimensional witness of
Theorem~\ref{thm:infinite-strict-upstream-separation} proves properness.
It is not replaced by a truncation or a weakened product comparison.
Alternatively set $\mu=0$ in this very native family and keep all the declared
protocols, grammar and rate-dependent calibrations. The same global theorem
still proves membership in $\Cop$, all required readings and denominators
remain defined, and the paired valuation responses are exactly equal.
This gives a complete countermodel in the same grammar with valuation zero,
independently of the older symmetric witness.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{A realized control for the additional scalar burden.}
The inequality $\lambda_\Phi>\lambda_G$ is not merely notational. On the same
plant define, prospectively, a second complete calibration with
$e=1/4096$. Soften the two index weights to $(1\pm e c_I/d_I)/2$ and the
ownership assignment to $(1+2eR_O)/2$. Keep the independent reference and
neutral control. The index and ownership scores are then e; the latter's
raw causal transplant has signed gain $-e$. Use
\[
 s_C=(1-e)/e,\qquad
 s_P=s_R=(179/240)/e-14/15,\qquad
 s_V=1-4385/262144.
\]
All scales are positive, and the canonical formulas give e for continuity,
perspective, valuation and irreducibility. The profile remains one when its
prospectively matched drop threshold is $e/2$. The four physical upstream
descriptors keep their raw clearances and predictive gains; ownership
coherence is at least e, still above its fixed threshold $1/16384$.
Thus the other defining clauses hold, with
\[
 \GateSubj=e>1/8192,\qquad
 \PhiSubj^7=e^6=2^{-72}<2^{-28}=(1/16)^7.
\]
This complete, differently calibrated model passes the gate and fails only
the scalar clause. Its parameters are part of its model data, not a
post-outcome rescaling offered as evidence of stronger organization.

\paragraph{Which burdens this theorem does not remove.}
With an unrestricted same-cost emulator the raw irreducibility margin is
nonpositive, the clipped coordinate is zero and the positive gate fails.
Ownership collapse or a broken privileged history likewise gives a zero
coordinate; rate symmetrization gives valuation zero, as the analytic response
formulas show. These are failures of this very conjunction, not missing data
silently filled with zeros. The result constructs an instrumented mathematical
member of the specified class. It does not prove that the two-port restriction
characterizes every non-subjective architecture or that the certificate implies
experience. Wider grammars and complete Paper IX transport/adjudication burdens
remain separate research problems.

\subsection{A Joint Rational Measurement Window}

Use a finite system with state $(z,b)\in\{0,1\}\times\{0,1\}^5$, where $z$ is a balanced exogenous context and $b=(b_I,b_O,b_C,b_P,b_V)$. Reset $b$ to all ones. On a one-step window a target $u_j$ sets only $b_j=0$; the sham $u_0$ preserves all bits. The context is unchanged. Use the same prepared contexts in every arm and error-free rational observations. All the coordinates below are functions of this one transition system and protocol.

In the order \emph{context label, other label}, the index weights are $((1+b_I)/2,(1-b_I)/2)$. The admissible within-run index relabelling is the identity. The index gap is $b_I$, including zero when the weights tie. For the two events, fix the reference $r_z=(1-z,z)$ by the assigned context. The candidate reports $r_z$ when $b_O=1$ and $(1/2,1/2)$ otherwise. The matched control always reports $(1/2,1/2)$, so the reference-based ownership gain is $b_O/2$. The declared continuity scores of the context line and its rival are $b_C$ and zero, giving $b_C/(1+b_C)$. These scores use a fixed context match on the two candidate lines.

The perspective outcome is the binary readout $b_Pz$. The indexed predictor uses $z$ and the command, while the index-neutral rival may use the command but receives no context proxy. Its best zero-one loss on the balanced population is $b_P/2$, and the indexed predictor has loss zero. The normalized perspective gain is $b_P/(2+b_P)$. For valuation, a probe of role $\ell\in\{0,1\}$ produces $b_V\mathbf1_{\ell=z}$. The matched roles $z$ and $1-z$ have equal budgets and give responses $b_V$ and zero, so the normalized asymmetry is $b_V/(1+b_V)$.

For irreducibility reserve separate balanced replicas with $u_0$ for baseline loss and $u_O$ for intervention loss. Both have $b_P=1$. The candidate and rivals receive the same command and predict the same declared selectivity pattern, giving zero selectivity-prediction loss; the complexity coefficient is fixed at zero. Against the declared finite weak family of all context-blind binary predictors by command, the candidate has total loss zero and the best rival has total loss $1/2+1/2=1$. Giving a rival the index is a separate tie control. The reserve is never used to compute the intervention profile.

\begin{proposition}[Joint Rational Certificate of the Seven Local Measurements]\label{prop:joint-rational-window}
For this window, with minimum target drop $1/4$, maximum absolute off-target change $1/8$, and zero allowed sham positives, the normalized measurements in the order of $\Substate$ are
\[
 (1,\tfrac12,\tfrac12,\tfrac13,\tfrac12,1,\tfrac12).
\]
The gate is $1/3$ and the functional with equal weights $1/7$ is $48^{-1/7}$. Both exceed the declared threshold $1/4$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
At reset, the five primary scores evaluate to the first five entries. Each of the five target transitions sets its designated score to zero and leaves all other primary scores unchanged, while the sham changes none. Thus each target drop is at least $1/3$, the off-target changes are zero and the intervention score is one. The reserved rival loss is one because two equiprobable contexts have opposite binary outcomes under each reserved command. No context-blind rule beats $1/2$ on either balanced block. Hence the irreducibility score is $(1-0)/(1+1)=1/2$. Multiplication gives the seventh power $1/48$, and the minimum is $1/3$. Since $48<4^7$, the functional exceeds $1/4$. Restoring the index to the weak predictor permits zero loss and forces a tie, as required by the negative control.
\end{proof}

This is a nonempty local measurement certificate, with the intervention and rival clauses verified on the declared finite protocol. Definition~\ref{def:subfp} also requires inherited Paper V and VII certificates. Those clauses remain separate obligations for full membership. The comparison establishes a gap relative to the specified context-blind predictors on these tasks.

\subsection{A Topological Obstruction to Native Lesions}

Use the circle-prefix tower of Papers V--VI \cite{paper5,paper6} on the safe band of Paper VII \cite{paper7}. Its states are $(p,\theta,b,m)$ with $\theta\in\mathbb T$, $b\in\{0,1\}^{\mathbb N_{>0}}$, and $m\in[-1/4,1/4]$. For all native commands $(u,q)$ the projected update on $(p,b)$ is $(p,Rb)$ with $Rb=(0,1-b_1,1-b_2,\ldots)$. The phase and memory updates depend on the commands.

\begin{theorem}[Connected-Fibre Obstruction to Native Discrete Lesions]\label{thm:tower-native-lesion-obstruction}
Suppose the sham and targets start from the same preparation, use only native commands, and are evaluated after the same number of transitions. Let their discrete word be assigned by a common continuous decoder from the whole safe support. The same hypothesis holds for the induced state decoder if it uses a common fixed finite diagnostic schedule with continuous clean observations and preserves the clean label under observation error of a uniformly positive radius. Then every target has the same word as the sham. In particular the five distinct native lesions required to reproduce the discrete switch protocol of Proposition~\ref{prop:joint-rational-window} cannot be realized under these conditions.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At fixed $(p,b)$ the fibre is homeomorphic to $\mathbb T\times[-1/4,1/4]$ and is connected. A continuous map from it to a discrete space is constant. In the diagnostic formulation, write $H(x)$ for the clean window starting at the state to be decoded. For $x'$ sufficiently close to $x$, continuity puts $H(x')$ inside the allowed noise ball around $H(x)$. Uniform robustness makes their decoded words identical, so the induced state decoder is locally constant and hence continuous. After $n$ native steps from one preparation, all arms have the same $(p,R^n b)$ even if the commands are chosen by feedback. Thus all arms end in one fibre. The finite switch protocol instead requires a one-step target word different from the sham word.
\end{proof}

Two fixed profiles can encode context. The five initial prefix bits can be read as preparation contrasts, but the common shift and complement carry them to positions $2$ through $6$ in every arm. A selective change to a prefix bit from a common preparation requires an intervention that changes the prefix update. Restricted diagnostic domains offer a different possibility because separated phase or memory regions need not be connected within the domain. Their evidence requires declared coverage and a rival with access to the same commands. For example, prepare $m=0$ and use $q=0$. The six commands $u=w/32$, where $w$ is the all-active five-bit word or one of its five single-bit lesions interpreted as an integer, give six separated phases $\theta'=u$. A rival given $u$ predicts these labels exactly. This control separates the existence of labels on a restricted domain from selective intervention on the system's own organization.

\subsection{Selective Lesions in the Writable Common Tower}

Use the writable circle-prefix tower of Paper V \cite{paper5}, with the same memory and safe-band recovery of Papers VI--VII \cite{paper6,paper7}. Associate sites $3$ through $7$ in order with index, ownership, continuity, perspective, and valuation. For each balanced context $z=0,1$, prepare $(p_z,0,b^\star,0)$. From that same preparation use a neutral sham or write the opposite of $b^\star$ at one designated site. On copies of the resulting state use common neutral role probes with zero base phase and no additional write. The diagnostic does not receive the lesion label. The next observed $r_0$ in turns, unwrapped near zero, is either $0$ or $\delta$. Thresholding at $\delta/2$ gives
\[
 Y_i(\ell)=A_i(b)\mathbf 1_{\{\ell=z\}}.
\]
This decoder is correct for phase error at most $\delta/4$; the code prefix and profile are also decodable within this budget. The scope is the prepared diagnostic windows with zero memory. The phase probe acts on the state transition.

\begin{proposition}[Five Selective Lesions on the Writable Common Tower]\label{prop:writable-tower-selective-lesions}
The five interventions each reduce their primary score by at least $1/4$, with zero changes off target and zero sham positives. The normalized vector in the order of $\Substate$ on the declared comparison is
\[
 (1,\tfrac12,\tfrac12,\tfrac13,\tfrac12,1,\tfrac12).
\]
Against the declared context-blind family the reserve loss gap is one, the gate is $1/3$, and the equal-weight functional is $48^{-1/7}>1/4$.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
In each block the tape after the sham is $b^\star$ and a target changes only its address. The phase and memory after that step agree across arms. For the index, form the two weights $(1+Y_i(\ell)-Y_i(1-\ell))/2$ from the index probe. Their gap is $A_i$. The within-run admissible relabeling fixes the roles. A joint renaming of roles and their references preserves the comparison. For ownership use the same two assignments from the ownership probe, with the reference fixed by the prepared role and a flat comparator with assignments $1/2$. The gain in mean absolute error is $A_i/2$. For continuity compare the responses of the matched profile line and its rival in the continuity probe, giving gap $A_i/(1+A_i)$. The valuation contrast uses the matched self and other probe roles and gives the same normalized formula at its own site.

For perspective predict $Y_i(1)$ from the measured pre-probe profile and bit. The candidate has loss zero. A predictor without the profile or a proxy for it receives identical data in the two balanced contexts, including the command, phase, tape and memory available to it. Its best zero-one loss is $A_i/2$, so the normalized perspective gain is $A_i/(2+A_i)$. Each score therefore changes only when its own site changes. The minimum target drop is $1/3$. All five targets meet the selectivity rule and its score is one. A write of the reference value gives the same control as the native sham from this preparation.

Use separate balanced replicas of the sham and ownership lesion for baseline and intervention loss in the reserve. Both have the perspective site active. The future phase outcome has opposite labels in the two contexts within each block. Candidate loss is zero. Every context-blind predictor, including mixtures based on the same information, has expected loss $1/2$ in each block. The complexity weight is zero and the declared selectivity pattern is predicted equally in both arms. The normalized gap is therefore $\widehat{\Irred}_{\mathrm{blind}}=1/2$. Multiplication gives $\PhiSubj^7=1/48$ and the minimum gives $\GateSubj=1/3$.
\end{proof}

The reserved loss comparison masks the relative phase channels for the blind family. Restoring these channels gives a rival zero loss because the profile is then decodable. Suppressing the write leaves all sites active and removes the target drops. Suppressing the probe feedback retains the tape changes and removes the phase response. At phase error $\delta/2$ the two clean levels can give the same reading. These controls locate the causal and information assumptions. The construction escapes the connected-fibre obstruction by changing the tape component of the fibre. The local margins hold against the declared information-restricted family. Full membership in Definition~\ref{def:subfp} still requires the inherited subjecthood evidence and a comparison against the admissible weak closure.

\subsection{A Held-Out Return Task with Informed Rivals}

The outcome here is a subsequent phase failure under the selected action. The five primary scores and the context-blind comparison above use their original trials. The return task uses a disjoint set of trials and neither the outcome nor the action that avoids it enters those scores.

Use the seven profiles of the writable tower, and form public strata $x=(p,\sigma,w)$ from profile $p$, assignment command $q=\sigma\in\{-1,1\}$ and one common write $w$. The write is either absent or is one of the five designated lesions. In each stratum prepare two copies of the phase-zero alternating state $b^\star$ and set the private memory to $m=s/8$, with $s=\pm1$ balanced. The public record includes that the perturbation occurred but carries no sign. Execute the assignment step with $u=0$, the indicated $q$ and $w$, and no probe. The decision time is immediately after this step. Apply a single chosen action $u=v/128$ with $v\in\{-1,1\}$, $q=0$, and neither a write nor a probe. The action fails if the next circular distance to phase zero exceeds $1/256$ turns, with terminal phase error bounded by $\eta=3^{-10}/4$. Both actions have cost $1/128$. There is also an abstention with loss $3/4$ and no action. An executed action has loss one on failure and zero on success.

All predictors receive the four current public readings, the same public command record, and the same calibration. Each may query at most one earlier public reading, retain at most one bit per trial, and fit at most four binary parameters from the calibration. No physical probe is available before the decision. Define the public rival class to contain all decision rules on these inputs that obey the budget, with optional randomization independent of the perturbation. The class includes arbitrary choices of which previous reading to query within the budget. For a fixed public stratum, all recorded readings through decision time agree between signs, so this choice never reveals the hidden sign.

Calibration uses the zero profile, no writes, and initial $m=s/16$. A neutral step gives prior phase $s/128$ and memory $s/32$, and the assignment step gives memory $\sigma s/64$. The two calibration ports $\pm1/512$ therefore identify the four-entry table $v=-\sigma s$ for the sign of that prior phase and the assignment command. The table is fixed before using the reserve amplitudes. A profile rule can use the parity of the final relative phase offset in units of $1/8$. A history rule applies the four-entry table to the queried prior phase and $\sigma$. The memory--policy hybrid stores the prior sign and applies the fitted multiplicative assignment factor, which here is $\sigma$. A zero cue has a fixed sign convention. The interacting comparator of Paper VI \cite{paper6} uses its own nonneutral rule, reversing the remembered sign under either nonneutral command. The candidate uses the tower's return rule with the same inputs and calibration. These rules are representatives of the public comparison. The obstruction concerns the entire admissible class.

\begin{theorem}[Public Information Obstruction on the Writable Tower Return Task]\label{thm:writable-tower-public-return-tie}
In each public stratum, the two hidden perturbations have identical public records and opposite unique successful action signs. Every admissible forced public rule has balanced failure risk $1/2$. At coverage $c$ the total loss is $1/2+(1-c)/4$, where coverage is the probability of executing an action. The infimum over the public class, including mixtures and their closure, equals $1/2$ and is attained. Thus the candidate's signed margin, defined as the public rival infimum minus candidate loss, is zero for every forced public candidate. The public randomized minimax risk over the two signs also equals $1/2$.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
At the assignment step the first tape digit is zero, so the two signs produce the same phase zero and the same tape after the common write. The unchanged profile and the absence of a phase response to memory at nonneutral $q$ give identical readings. The hidden memories after assignment are $\sigma s/16$. With neutral $q$ the terminal phase has signed representative $(v+\sigma s)/128$. The two distances to zero are therefore $0$ and $1/64$. Since $\eta<1/256$ and $1/64-\eta>1/256$, the unique success is $v=-\sigma s$ throughout the error range. These relations use the native return channel. Neither the phase probe nor the five score readouts is needed.

The public transcript, including any permissible previous query and calibration, has the same value for both signs in a stratum. Conditional on executing a given action, the sign remains balanced, because all internal randomization is independent of the perturbation. Each forced action then has risk $1/2$. Averaging over action and abstention gives the formula. Each stratum admits a fixed forced rule and the resulting risk bound survives mixtures and limits of action distributions. For the worst-sign criterion the balanced risk gives the lower bound, and a fair forced coin has error $1/2$ under either sign.
\end{proof}

Interchanging $\sigma$ interchanges the winning ports at fixed $s$. Setting $m=0$ makes both ports yield distance $1/128$, which exceeds the failure threshold even after the allowed error. For a history control, start at $m=s/4$, then take a neutral step before the assignment. It gives a public previous phase of $s/32$ and leaves $m=s/8$ before the assignment. The current readings are again identical across signs after assignment, but one query of that previous reading resolves the sign. The calibrated candidate, the history rule and the hybrid can each attain zero loss on this control. In contrast the Paper VI interacting comparator succeeds at $\sigma=-1$ and fails at $\sigma=+1$ because it predicts a sign inversion in both cases. This mismatch identifies a specific dynamical comparator that loses even though the best public rival still ties the candidate.

\begin{theorem}[Exact Separation by an Accessible Private Signal]\label{thm:writable-tower-private-return-separation}
Retain the hidden return task of Theorem~\ref{thm:writable-tower-public-return-tie}. Let the candidate alone receive a finite signal $Z$ at decision time, with laws $\mu_x^+$ and $\mu_x^-$ conditional on the public stratum $x$ and the successful action signs $+1$ and $-1$, respectively. Assume the signal is available in one permitted reading and the decision maps used for the stated optima can be fixed from the declared signal law within the budget, without fitting on the reserve. For the worst-sign optimum this includes the allowed randomization from a fixed map of the reading to an action probability. The predictor retains only its sampled binary decision. For stratum weights $w_x$, the exact best signed margin against the public rival closure is
\[
 \sup_{\text{candidate}}\inf_{\text{public rival}}(R_{\mathrm{rival}}-R_{\mathrm{candidate}})
 = \frac12 \sum_x w_x\,\operatorname{TV}(\mu_x^+,\mu_x^-),
 \qquad
 \operatorname{TV}(\mu,\nu)=\frac12\sum_z|\mu(z)-\nu(z)|.
\]
It is positive exactly when the two accessible laws differ on a positive-weight stratum.

Under minimax over the two successful action signs at fixed $x$, allowing randomization, the best private risk is
\[
 \max_{0\le\pi\le1}\sum_z
       \min\{\pi\mu_x^+(z),(1-\pi)\mu_x^-(z)\}.
\]
It is strictly below the public minimax risk $1/2$ exactly when $\mu_x^+\ne\mu_x^-$. For a uniform worst-sign improvement across the finitely many strata, each pair of accessible laws must differ and their respective optimal rules must be jointly admissible. If the same signal and decision budget are shared with the rivals, their closure can reproduce the candidate decision and the best signed margin is zero.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Replacing any abstention by an independent fair forced action changes its conditional loss from $3/4$ to $1/2$ under either sign. Thus abstention cannot improve either optimum. In the balanced stratum, choosing the sign with larger likelihood at each $z$ attains error
\[
 \frac12\sum_z\min\{\mu_x^+(z),\mu_x^-(z)\}
 =\frac12\bigl(1-\operatorname{TV}(\mu_x^+,\mu_x^-)\bigr).
\]
No mixture of actions lowers the smaller conditional loss. This optimal binary decision is stored in one bit. Averaging over $x$ and subtracting from the public infimum proves the balanced formula.

For the worst-sign criterion the set of randomized rules on the finite signal alphabet is compact and convex. The conditional risks for the two signs are linear in the rule. The finite minimax theorem equates the best worst-sign error with the maximum, over the prior weight $\pi$ of sign $+1$, of the best $\pi$-weighted error. For each $\pi$ the best error selects the smaller of the two weighted likelihoods pointwise, giving the displayed value. The sum of minima is at most $\min\{\pi,1-\pi\}$, strictly below $1/2$ away from $\pi=1/2$. At $\pi=1/2$ the balanced formula is strictly below $1/2$ if the laws differ. Continuity on the compact prior interval makes the maximum strictly below $1/2$ in that case. If the laws coincide, the balanced prior attains $1/2$. The rule can retain only its sampled binary decision. For both criteria the full public class with a shared signal includes the same rule under the matched budget. In particular its infimum equals the candidate optimum under symmetric access.
\end{proof}

For the private copy of the memory sign, the conditional laws have disjoint supports, so the gain is $1/2$ for both criteria. For a copy that is independently erased with probability $1/2$, the two laws share only the erased outcome with mass $1/2$. The balanced and minimax risks are then both $1/4$ and the gain is $1/4$. An accessible signal computed solely as a deterministic function of the common public prefix and commands has identical laws in the two branches. Such a signal cannot provide strict separation for the task above. A dynamical coupling would have to expose information correlated with the winning port that the candidate can use within budget and the public class cannot access, or establish a specific asymmetry of realizable decision rules. Exposing the same information to both sides improves their attainable losses together. The exact separation obtained here is relative to the declared information channel, while the five-lesion diagnostic retains its distinct comparison.

\section{Intervention-to-artifact mapping and failure semantics}
The implementation burden becomes much clearer when each intervention is mapped to artifacts and failure codes.

\subsection{Swap family}
The self/non-self swap intervention should at minimum emit:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \texttt{\detokenize{self_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{nonself_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{swap_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{report}}
\item updated \texttt{\detokenize{self_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{index_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{state}}
\item updated \texttt{\detokenize{ownershi}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{p_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{attribut}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ion_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{report}}
\item updated \texttt{\detokenize{first_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{person_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{perspect}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ive_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{report}}
\end{enumerate}
Expected failure codes include \texttt{\detokenize{swap_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{invarian}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{t}}, \texttt{\detokenize{swap_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{nonselec}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{tive}}, and \texttt{\detokenize{swap_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{control_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{confound}}.

\subsection{Ownership family}
Ownership lesions should emit both a local report and an updated gate result. The decisive question is whether ownness loss propagates selectively into the gate or whether it behaves like arbitrary logging damage. Relevant failure codes include \texttt{\detokenize{ownershi}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{p_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{decorati}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ve}} and \texttt{\detokenize{ownershi}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{p_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{aliasing}}.

\subsection{Continuity family}
Continuity breaks and auto\-bio\-graphical remaps should emit:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \texttt{\detokenize{subjecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ve_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{continui}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ty_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{report}}
\item fork or fracture subreports
\item updated \texttt{\detokenize{subjecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{vity_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{runtime_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{projecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on}}
\end{enumerate}
Relevant failure codes include \texttt{\detokenize{continui}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ty_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{flat}}, \texttt{\detokenize{memory_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{only_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{explanat}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ion}}, and \texttt{\detokenize{fork_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{underdet}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ermined}}.

\subsection{Perspective and valuation family}
Perspective flattening and valuation perturbations should jointly update the perspective and valuation reports, because in a serious subjectivity stack those layers should interact. If one changes dramatically while the other remains entirely invariant, the implementation should explicitly say so. Relevant failure codes include \texttt{\detokenize{perspect}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ive_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{optional}} and \texttt{\detokenize{valuatio}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{n_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{generic}}.

\subsection{Cross-substrate family}
Cross-substrate remaps should emit a dedicated transport report including:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item preserved coordinates;
\item failed coordinates;
\item transport class: preserving, fractured, forked, or unsupported;
\item winning weak rival under the remap if the stronger model fails.
\end{enumerate}
This report is essential because cross-substrate claims are otherwise too easy to overread.

\subsection{Failure semantics}
The artifact layer should localize failure by cause, not merely by score. A strong minimal taxonomy is:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \texttt{\detokenize{missing_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{coordina}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{te_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{evidence}}
\item \texttt{\detokenize{weak_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{model_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{sufficie}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{nt}}
\item \texttt{\detokenize{interven}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{tion_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{nonselec}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{tive}}
\item \texttt{\detokenize{control_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{confound}}
\item \texttt{\detokenize{fork_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ambiguit}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{y}}
\item \texttt{\detokenize{unsuppor}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ted_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{transpor}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{t}}
\item \texttt{\detokenize{admissib}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ility_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{broken}}
\end{enumerate}
Such a taxonomy matters scientifically because it turns a failed subjectivity claim into information about what exactly failed, instead of reducing every failure to a vague ``not enough evidence'' label.

\section{Negative controls, ablation matrix, and causal discrimination protocol}
The main text specifies the intervention family. A serious subjectivity program also needs a systematic negative-control and ablation protocol. Without it, a clever but weak implementation could produce superficially impressive artifacts while remaining reducible to complexity, bookkeeping, or narrative fit.

\subsection{Negative control families}
At minimum, the following controls should be implemented:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item \textbf{complexity-matched non-indexed controls:} systems with comparable scale and internal richness but no privileged self-index;
\item \textbf{memory-rich non-owning controls:} systems with extensive auto\-bio\-graphical storage but flat ownership assignment;
\item \textbf{policy-matched controls:} agents with strong reward optimization but no subject-level continuity burden;
\item \textbf{narrative-rich controls:} systems capable of sophisticated self-description without interventional subjectivity structure;
\item \textbf{bookkeeping controls:} systems whose self-like outputs are produced by latent tags and registries only.
\end{enumerate}
The role of these controls is not merely comparative. They define the nearest ways in which the theory could be wrong while still looking superficially plausible.

\subsection{Ablation targets}
Every constitutive coordinate should be tied to at least one clean ablation family:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item self-index ablations through candidate-locus symmetrization;
\item ownership ablations through ownness-field flattening;
\item continuity ablations through fork or fracture operators;
\item perspective ablations through index-neutral factorization;
\item valuation ablations through self/non-self reward equalization;
\item intervention-profile ablations through targeted-to-random perturbation substitution;
\item irreducibility ablations through weak-rival reconstruction challenges.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Ablation matrix}
\begin{tabularx}{\textwidth}{@{}p{0.18\textwidth}p{0.22\textwidth}p{0.24\textwidth}Y@{}}
\toprule
\textbf{Ablation} & \textbf{Primary expected drop} & \textbf{What weak rivals predict} & \textbf{Why the test matters} \\
\midrule
self-index symmetrization & $\widehat{\SelfIndex}_\tau$, then $\widehat{\Persp}_\tau$ & many weak rivals predict little more than relabeling cost & tests whether one locus is constitutively privileged \\
ownership flattening & $\widehat{\OwnField}_\tau$, then $\widehat{\ValAsym}_\tau$ & bookkeeping and narrative systems may remain mostly intact & separates ownness from self-description \\
continuity fracture & $\widehat{\ContField}_\tau$, then gate collapse & memory systems predict a weaker or purely archival effect & separates auto\-bio\-graphical continuity from stored content \\
perspective neutralization & $\widehat{\Persp}_\tau$ with downstream valuation impact & workspace models predict weaker downstream consequences & tests whether perspective is constitutive \\
valuation equalization & $\widehat{\ValAsym}_\tau$ and some gate degradation & policy models often absorb this into reward reshaping & separates self-valued organization from generic utility \\
weak-rival reconstruction & $\widehat{\Irred}_\tau$ & if weak rivals succeed, the stronger model collapses & keeps the theory honest \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabularx}

\subsection{Noise and adversarial perturbations}
The paper should also survive a stronger class of tests than clean lesions. Three families are especially important.

\paragraph{Noise injection.}
Small stochastic perturbations should not destroy the entire subjectivity profile unless they specifically target a constitutive coordinate. Otherwise the paper would define a class too brittle to be scientifically useful.

\paragraph{Adversarial tag attacks.}
The system should be exposed to perturbations specifically designed to fake self-like artifacts by manipulating surface tags, report channels, or local memory labels. If the stronger model cannot resist such attacks, it is not yet stronger than bookkeeping.

\paragraph{Counterfactual decoy insertion.}
One should inject near-self decoys into the candidate self-index pool. A robust subjectivity layer should not collapse merely because the environment offers a decoy with superficial similarity. This test is especially important for separating true self-index stability from report-conditioned heuristics.

\subsection{Causal discrimination protocol}
The correct order of causal testing is:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item measure baseline coordinates;
\item run negative controls;
\item run single-coordinate ablations;
\item run compound ablations designed to mimic weak-rival hypotheses;
\item compare against the weak family under the same intervention budget;
\item only then compute the final gate and irreducibility outputs.
\end{enumerate}
This ordering matters because a gate result computed before the weak-rival and control budget is exhausted is too easy to overread.

\subsection{Reproducibility burden}
No subjectivity program should be trusted if it cannot survive reruns. At minimum, the following reproducibility burdens should be imposed:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item seed schedule variation with fixed architecture;
\item comparator reruns with blinded weak-rival selection;
\item independent regeneration of gate results from stored artifacts;
\item cross-machine replay of the intervention harness;
\item explicit documentation of any run that fails admissibility before subjectivity is even evaluated.
\end{enumerate}
These burdens are not secondary engineering details. They are part of what distinguishes a scientific subjectivity program from a compelling one-off demonstration.

