\item \textbf{contamination event}: any unauthorized dataset population, evidence-bearing log entry, or decision-bearing record.
\item \textbf{abort event}: any operator-controlled termination of the controlled opening before collection start.
\item \textbf{invalidated session}: any session whose pre-collection boundary, blinding route, or provenance cleanliness is breached.
\end{itemize}

These control classes bind the transition-ready protocol shells, establishing
contamination and abort rules as execution-opening constraints rather than
unattached policy tokens.

\section{Operator Checklist}

The operator checklist governs execution readiness by binding the local
frozen record route, live manifest route, empty-log route, empty-dataset
state, contamination rules, reset rules, and explicit confirmation that no
evidence exists. Until it is complete, Tier P6 must not move from
\texttt{\detokenize{session_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{zero_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{ready}} to \texttt{\detokenize{collecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{openable_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{under_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{controls}}.

\section{Collection-Start Boundary}

\texttt{\detokenize{collecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{open_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{pre_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{evidenti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{al}} is not \texttt{\detokenize{collecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{started}}.
The boundary is crossed only by a later real operator-authorized collection
start event with provenance, contamination-free status, and a first admissible
log event.

\section{Evidence-Generation Boundary}

Evidence generation begins only when a real admissible population event enters
the human comparator dataset family, matched intervention family,
negative-control family, collection log, reproducibility package, or
decision-linked provenance chain. No Tier P6 pre-evidential shell instantiated under
this specification crosses that boundary.

\section{Decision-Record Boundary}

The live decision-record stub is an execution-opening shell only. It is not a
decision, not an adjudicator action, not a reviewer action, and not an
outcome. The protocol preserves that distinction because a populated
decision record belongs to a later evidential and adjudicative state family.

\section{Transition from Session-Zero to First Admissible Collection}

The transition from readiness to collection obeys five ordered steps:

\begin{enumerate}
\item session-zero becomes ready;
\item the pre-collection gate opens collection under controls;
\item Tier P6 opens a live but still pre-evidential state;
\item a later explicit collection-start command instantiates the first admissible collection event;
\item only then may the first dataset-population event exist.
\end{enumerate}

\section{Executable Integrity Verification}

Tier P6B adds an executable-integrity verification layer to confirm that the
declared implementation environment executes cleanly under reproducible rules.
The required audit surfaces include:

\begin{itemize}
\item toolchain visibility;
\item deterministic dependency installation under the declared package route;
\item full build behavior;
\item test suite behavior;
\item bounded local-start behavior;
\item verifier-chain behavior for source-of-truth, comparative closure, Tier P6, Tier P5, and Paper 9 handoff.
\end{itemize}

This verification step may pass with compatibility warnings such as engine
version differences or dependency advisories. A warning-bearing pass remains
operationally informative without crossing the boundary into empirical evidence.

\section{Clean-Room Reproducibility}

Tier P6B also specifies a clean-room reproducibility check asking whether an
isolated environment can reinstall dependencies, rebuild the package, rerun
key verifiers, and regenerate protocol records without relying on hidden
machine-specific state. Clean-room reproducibility may pass with warnings when a
documented compatibility route is used, provided the build remains verifiable.

\section{Dependency and Build Assumptions}

The executable-integrity layer makes dependency and build assumptions explicit:

\begin{itemize}
\item the declared execution engine forms part of the reproducibility contract;
\item deterministic locked installation remains the preferred setup route;
\item a fallback install route may be recorded only as a compatibility surface;
\item build success must remain distinct from evidence generation;
\item test success must remain distinct from adjudication.
\end{itemize}

\section{Reproducible Record Regeneration}

Tier P6B adds a source-driven record-regeneration check verifying whether
protocol records can be deterministically regenerated from source definitions
and classifying any differences as:

\begin{itemize}
\item absent;
\item provenance-only;
\item semantically stale; or
\item blocking.
\end{itemize}

Because external-adjudication readiness relies on an extensive family of
structured schemas, the audit separates semantic drift from ordinary timestamp
or lineage variation and rejects manual editing as a substitute for deterministic
generation.

\section{Local Path Leakage Policy}

Clean-room readiness requires that live normative protocol surfaces remain free
of machine-specific absolute paths. Historical planning records, when archived,
must remain explicitly marked as non-normative.

\section{Controlled Execution Opening Audit}

Tier P6B audits the controlled-execution opening layer itself without replacing
Tier P6, confirming that the live unpopulated records, session-zero gate, and
pre-collection gate survive executable verification and clean-room
reconstruction.

\section{Session-Zero Reproducibility}

Session-zero reproducibility requires that an isolated environment reconstruct the
session-zero manifest, operator checklist state, and failure-mode assessment
without hidden local inputs and without emitting collection-start or
evidence-bearing records.

\section{Pre-Collection Gate Reproducibility}

Pre-collection gate reproducibility requires that an isolated environment reconstruct the
pre-collection admissibility result, contamination checklist, and
invalidation status while preserving:

\begin{itemize}
\item \texttt{\detokenize{collecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{not_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{started}};
\item \texttt{\detokenize{evidence_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{generati}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{absent}};
\item \texttt{\detokenize{adjudica}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{tion_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{not_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{open}}.
\end{itemize}

\section{Why Reproducibility Does Not Imply Evidence}

Reproducibility shows that the formal and executable surfaces are rebuildable.
It does not show that any dataset has been populated, that any human evidence
exists, that any reviewer or adjudicator has acted, or that any result-bearing
section is licensed.

\section{Why Execution Health Does Not Imply Adjudication}

Execution health shows that the software and verification pathways operate reliably.
It does not imply that collection has started, that evidence has been generated,
that reviewer-governed interpretation exists, or that adjudication is open. A
healthy execution environment remains pre-evidential until real collection events
occur.

\section{Transition Conditions for First Admissible Collection}

Tier P7 may be classified as \texttt{\detokenize{first_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{collecti}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{on_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{openable}}
only when executable integrity and clean-room reproducibility pass verification,
normative surfaces remain free of path leakage, record regeneration is
non-blocking, Tier P6 remains openable, and no uncollected empirical results are
