\section{Comparator Design and Alternative Explanations}
\subsection{Comparator Rationale}
Comparator choice targets distinct explanations for apparent coherence:
reactive prompting, memory-augmented text continuity, dynamics without self-model, and internal policy ablation.

\subsection{Mandatory Baseline Policy (Hard Requirement)}
For each key claim experiment, the following baselines are mandatory:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{Heuristic baseline} (no world model, no planner),
\item \textbf{Planner-off} (world model ON),
\item \textbf{World-model-off} (planner ON with heuristic transition proxy).
\end{enumerate}
A claim without these contrasts is inadmissible for tier upgrade.

\subsection{Internal Falsifier Role of Ablation}
\begin{corollary}
If QICN FULL does not reliably exceed ABLATION QICN POLICY OFF, integration-level claims are weakened and cannot be escalated.
\end{corollary}

\subsection{Comparator Adequacy}
The comparator set is adequate only if each alternative removes or weakens a
different explanatory ingredient while preserving enough task exposure to make the
contrast meaningful. A weak comparator that cannot perform the task at all is a
sanity check, not a discriminating baseline. A strong comparator that reproduces
the task but lacks one frozen ingredient is more valuable because it places
pressure on the claimed necessity of that ingredient.

\begin{definition}[Comparator Coverage Vector]
For a comparator $s_i$, define
\[
V(s_i)=(v_A,v_J,v_W,v_M,v_P),
\]
where $v_A$ records actuator coverage, $v_J$ objective compatibility, $v_W$
workspace compatibility, $v_M$ memory/model access, and $v_P$ planning access.
Each coordinate is reported as \texttt{full}, \texttt{restricted}, or
\texttt{absent}.
\end{definition}

\begin{criterion}[Non-Trivial Comparator]
A comparator is non-trivial for a prediction $P_k$ only if it has enough coverage
to attempt the target scenario while lacking at least one ingredient that the
prediction treats as structurally relevant.
\end{criterion}

\subsection{Negative-Control Acceptance}
Negative controls have a different success condition from positive runs. A
negative control succeeds scientifically when it exposes whether the pipeline can
withhold support from a superficially attractive but structurally defective case.

\begin{definition}[Negative-Control Pass]
A negative control passes the evaluation when the evaluator correctly withholds
the target claim despite high activity, high fluency, high connectivity, or high
surface task performance.
\end{definition}

\begin{remark}
The strongest negative controls are not intentionally broken systems. They are
near-plausible alternatives that preserve enough surface competence to make false
promotion tempting.
\end{remark}

\section{Statistical Inference Specification}
\subsection{Primary Metrics First (Anti p-hacking)}
\begin{protocol}[Analysis Lock]
Inference order is frozen:
\begin{enumerate}
\item evaluate primary metrics only,
\item if primaries fail: global result fails,
\item secondary metrics are descriptive and cannot overturn failed primaries.
\end{enumerate}
\end{protocol}

\subsection{Power Planning and Minimum Sample Size}
\begin{assumption}
Final claims require pre-registered minimum sample size per comparator/scenario sufficient for target power $\ge 0.8$ under conservative effect assumptions.
\end{assumption}

\subsection{Multiplicity and Effect Size}
Holm correction is applied within endpoint families; in addition, each accepted contrast must pass a practical effect-size floor (pre-registered).

\subsection{Decision Semantics}
A prediction counts as supported iff directionality, integrity gate, multiplicity-corrected significance, and effect-size floor all hold.

\subsection{Hierarchical Decision Rule}
The protocol uses hierarchical decisions because not all failures have the same
meaning. The gate order is:
\[
\begin{aligned}
\text{integrity} \rightarrow \text{eligibility} \rightarrow
\text{primary direction}&\rightarrow \text{effect size}\\
&\rightarrow\text{multiplicity} \rightarrow \text{secondary description}.
\end{aligned}
\]
No later stage can repair an earlier failure.

\begin{protocol}[Cell-Level Decision]
For each system-scenario family, compute:
\[
\Delta_{i0} = \hat{\mu}(s_1,c)-\hat{\mu}(s_i,c),
\]
where $s_1$ is QICN FULL and $s_i$ is the comparator. The cell is supportive only
if the admissible packet set passes integrity, $\Delta_{i0}$ has the
pre-registered direction, the confidence interval excludes the practical
irrelevance band, and the family survives multiplicity correction.
\end{protocol}

\begin{definition}[Practical Irrelevance Band]
For endpoint $e$, let $\tau_e>0$ be the smallest effect regarded as scientifically
meaningful before execution. A contrast inside $[-\tau_e,\tau_e]$ is reported as
inconclusive for support even if a large sample makes the numerical difference
statistically detectable.
\end{definition}

\subsection{Missingness and Exclusion Accounting}
Every missing packet has evidentiary meaning. The protocol therefore separates:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \textbf{structural missingness}, caused by scenario infeasibility or runner
failure;
\item \textbf{forensic exclusion}, caused by integrity failure;
\item \textbf{budget exclusion}, caused by runtime or latency violation;
\item \textbf{analysis exclusion}, caused by a pre-registered endpoint rule.
\end{enumerate}
The final table must report counts for all four classes. A favorable result with
unexplained missingness is not admissible for escalation.

\section{Independent Campaign Units and Exact Failure Controls}

\subsection{Frozen Population, Noise and Decision}

The inferential unit is an independently drawn preparation block, with both systems evaluated under the same seed, intervention, acquisition schedule and predeclared scoring map. All ticks, repeated renderings and coupled replicas of that preparation belong to the block. Independent blocks have independent assignments and disturbances. Define the paired loss difference $D_i=L_{i,\mathrm{rival}}-L_{i,\mathrm{candidate}}\in[-1,1]$, the population mean $\Delta=\mathbb E D_i$ and the primary win probability $\pi=\Pr(W_i=1)$ for a fixed rule $W_i$. These are different estimands. A sign-based result alone does not identify $\Delta$.

Freeze the population, identifiers, pairing, horizon, threshold, randomization, calibration and exclusion map before unmasking outcomes. Readout errors may be dependent within a block. If the two loss estimates each have certified error at most $\eta$, then $|\widehat D_i-D_i|\le2\eta$. A declared win rule $\widehat D_i>2\eta+\tau$, for a fixed $\tau\ge0$, guarantees $D_i>\tau$. The probabilistic null for that rule must be specified independently. Exclusion requires an integrity rule invariant to arm exchange and hidden labels, applied before inspecting the endpoint. Every planned block retains a disposition in the audit trail. An insufficient number of eligible blocks gives an unsupported campaign. Future campaigns use these frozen units for inference, with preliminary synthetic tables retaining their descriptive status.

\begin{theorem}[Exact Independent-Block Win Test and Power]\label{thm:campaign-block-sign-test}
Suppose $W_1,\ldots,W_n$ are independent Bernoulli variables and $\Pr(W_i=1)\le1/2$ under the sign null. For a fixed $k$, the rejection probability of $\sum_i W_i\ge k$ is at most
\[
 B_{n,k}(1/2)=2^{-n}\sum_{j=k}^n {n\choose j}.
\]
For iid success probability $\pi$ the power is exactly $B_{n,k}(\pi)=\sum_{j=k}^n{n\choose j}\pi^j(1-\pi)^{n-j}$. With $n=20$, $\alpha=1/20$ and $k=15$, the size is at most $5425/262144<\alpha$ and the power at $\pi=4/5$ exceeds $4/5$. The threshold $14$ fails the size requirement.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
Couple each indicator to an independent uniform variable. Raising each success probability to $1/2$ can only raise the increasing event, whose probability is the binomial tail. Under the stated iid alternative the same enumeration gives the power formula. Substitution gives the exact rational inequalities. Under a sharp no-effect null, independently randomized arm exchange makes the paired score sign conditionally symmetric; ties assigned to non-wins are conservative. The sign null also admits other specified Bernoulli models. An average-effect null for $\Delta$ alone does not imply that sign null.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Exact Inflation under Three Auditable Violations}

\begin{proposition}[Leakage, Pseudoreplication and Selected-Window Inflation]\label{prop:campaign-pathology-inflation}
The following synthetic null traces have exact false-positive rates under their stated generation laws.
\begin{enumerate}
\item A frozen blind predictor matching five independent fair labels in all five cases passes with probability $1/32$. Copying the held-out label, including through a calibration table keyed by a reused held-out unit identifier, passes with probability one.
\item Three independent fair signs repeated four times each and analyzed as twelve signs pass the nominal ten-of-twelve rule with probability $1/8$. The incorrectly reported iid tail is $79/4096$. The three-block test at $\alpha=1/20$ never rejects.
\item For two independent five-sign windows, declaring success if either window is all positive has probability $63/1024$. A single preselected window has probability $1/32$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
In the first trace, independent matches multiply to $2^{-5}$, while the leaked predictor is correct by construction. In the second trace the possible twelve-counts are $0,4,8,12$, so the naive rule rejects exactly when the three independent signs are all positive. With three genuine units the smallest possible upper-tail probability is $1/8>\alpha$. In the third trace the two success events have probability $1/32$ independently, so their union has probability $1-(31/32)^2=63/1024>\alpha$. More generally, selecting any of $K$ independent tests of exact size $p$ gives size $1-(1-p)^K$. Without their independence the union bound still gives size at most $Kp$. A predeclared joint maximum statistic can instead be evaluated under its full joint randomization law, or a fixed family can use its predeclared multiplicity allocation.
\end{proof}

An audit rejects the displayed leaked trace when calibration and reserve identifiers overlap or when a counterfactual change of a hidden label alone changes the output of a supposedly blind frozen predictor. Repeated unit identifiers expose the duplicated-block trace. A decision window absent from the frozen protocol or an unrecorded stopping opportunity invalidates the selected-window promotion. These rejection rules require the complete provenance record. Observed output accuracy alone cannot certify absence of covert leakage. Excluding a failed block after observing its score or choosing a new threshold requires a fresh confirmatory campaign or a separately justified selective-inference procedure.

\subsection{A Typed External Experimental Interface}\label{sec:external-interface-contract}

\begin{definition}[Experimental interface contract]\label{def:external-experimental-interface}
A prospective external test specifies a tuple
\[
 \mathcal E=(\mathsf P,\mathsf B,\mathsf X,\mathsf U,\mathsf Y,
 C_{\rm obs},\mathsf K,\mathsf D,\mathsf M,\mathsf L,\mathsf J).
\]
Here $\mathsf P$ is a population and preparation law, with inclusion criteria fixed before outcomes; $\mathsf B$ defines genuinely independent experimental units and dependence within each block; $\mathsf X$ is the candidate state model; $\mathsf U$ is the sensory/motor intervention specification, including target, matched sham, causal disconnection and restoration families; and $\mathsf Y$ supplies the measurement channels, sampling intervals, units and missing-data rules. The observation map $C_{\rm obs}$ and uncertainty kernel $\mathsf K$ bind model responses to measurements. The decoder/calibration object $\mathsf D$ is trained outside the confirmatory population, blinded to held-out assignments and frozen with a reproducible identifier. Matching $\mathsf M$ fixes amplitude, energy, timing, information, actuator cost and nuisance covariates; the loss/effect object $\mathsf L$ fixes signs, scales, decision thresholds, populations and multiplicity. Finally $\mathsf J$ records the causal graph or structural assumptions that identify the intervention contrast. Unavailable fields yield an unevaluable interface, not an unfavorable biological result.

A biological realization must supply these fields through independent measurement and domain-specific methodological review. No organism, sensory modality, safe operating range or valid disconnection operator is identified by the abstract tuple alone. A biological sham is a realized manipulation matching the specified non-causal pathways while leaving the nominated mechanism intact; merely assigning a ``sham'' label does not establish that property. A disconnection test must preserve the measurement channel and declared comparator information, with off-target effects quantified. Restoration is a separate control of the claimed mechanism, not an automatic inference from survival or recovery.
\end{definition}

\paragraph{Causal and blinding assumptions.}
One admissible design uses independently randomized block assignments $A_i$, block-level preparation $Z_i$, motor/sensory perturbation $U_{it}$, state law $X_{i,t+1}=F(X_{it},U_{it},A_i,Z_i)$ and observation $Y_{it}=C_{\rm obs}(X_{it})+\xi_{it}$. Assignment has no arrow into the frozen decoder or its calibration reference. Its arrows into outcomes are the declared mechanism and measured nuisance pathways. No interference between independent blocks, consistency of implemented interventions, positivity of their assignment probabilities and the stated exclusion/matching assumptions are required for the corresponding causal interpretation. Randomization alone does not prove that disconnection has no off-target pathway. If these assumptions fail, only the appropriately qualified measurement contrast remains identified.

\begin{theorem}[A Calibrated Blockwise Falsation Bound]\label{thm:external-interface-falsation-bound}
For a frozen family of $m\ge1$ directional contrasts, let independent block vectors have scores $D_{ik}\in[-1,1]$. Independence between coordinates $k$ is not required. Suppose $\mu_k=n^{-1}\sum_i\mathbb E D_{ik}\ge\eta_k$ is a prediction of the declared realized model; identically distributed blocks are not required. Assume uniform readout calibration error at most $e$ on an event of probability at least $1-\beta$, $0\le\beta\le1$, and score Lipschitz constant $L$ for each arm, so the recorded contrast differs from its ideal score by at most $2Le$. Let $0<\alpha<1$ and
\[
 t_n=\sqrt{\frac{2\log(m/\alpha)}{n}}.
\]
The rule rejecting the joint directional prediction when some recorded mean is below $\eta_k-t_n-2Le$ has false rejection probability at most $\min(1,\alpha+\beta)$. If the actual decision can be coupled to this specified blind design so that it agrees outside a failure event of probability at most $p$, the bound is at most $\min(1,\alpha+\beta+p)$. An audit pass fraction alone does not establish that coupling.

For an alternative with some fixed $\mu_k\le\eta_k-g$, let $0<\zeta<1$ and $t_\zeta=\sqrt{2\log(1/\zeta)/n}$. If $g>t_n+t_\zeta+4Le$, rejection probability is at least $\max(0,1-\zeta-\beta-p)$ under the same coupling. For $g>4Le$, it suffices that
\[
 n>\frac{\bigl(\sqrt{2\log(m/\alpha)}+\sqrt{2\log(1/\zeta)}\bigr)^2}{(g-4Le)^2}.
\]
These are design-conditional guarantees, not a universal biological sample size.
\end{theorem}
\begin{proof}
On the calibration event, a rejection implies an ideal sample mean below $\eta_k-t_n$. For completeness, if $D\in[-1,1]$ and $\psi(s)=\log\mathbb E\exp(s(D-\mathbb ED))$, then $\psi(0)=\psi'(0)=0$ and $\psi''(s)$ is a tilted variance bounded by one. Integration gives $\psi(s)\le s^2/2$. Multiplying the exponential moments of independent blocks and using Markov's inequality with $s=t$ bounds either tail of the mean by $\exp(-nt^2/2)$; identical distributions are unnecessary. Thus the lower deviation probability is at most $\exp(-nt_n^2/2)=\alpha/m$ for each contrast. A union bound over the fixed family gives $\alpha$. Add the calibration-failure probability, and the coupling-failure probability when needed. For the alternative, except on an upper-tail event of probability at most $\zeta$, its ideal mean is at most $\eta_k-g+t_\zeta$. Calibration can increase the recorded mean by at most $2Le$; the displayed gap therefore puts it strictly below $\eta_k-t_n-2Le$. Another union bound proves the power bound. Squaring the positive headroom inequality gives the stated sufficient $n$. No independence of the failure events is assumed. Independence is between the declared blocks, not between repeated readings inside a block; replacing $n$ by the number of correlated samples is not justified.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Signal, controls and decisions.}
Specify measurement error in each channel's units before quoting a signal/noise ratio. The term $2Le$ transports that error to the dimensionless contrast; changing units requires transporting $L$ and $e$ together. A population expectation, a uniform error domain and a calibrated noise tail are different guarantees. Target/sham and target/disconnection contrasts should share costs and usable measurements. Include a restoration contrast, label-shuffled controls and a measurement-only dropout control: deleting the sensor cannot by itself identify loss of the latent mechanism. Preregister multiplicity, exclusions and stopping before outcomes. The theorem is conditional on the specified independent-unit design; it does not turn a synthetic power calculation into biological planning evidence.

\begin{proposition}[Observation Alone Cannot Identify the Mechanism]\label{prop:external-interface-nonidentifiability}
If a candidate and a rival induce identical laws of all admissible recorded histories and assignments, every frozen decision rule has the same rejection probability for both. Thus no such rule can empirically separate their latent mechanisms on that interface.
\end{proposition}
\begin{proof}
The decision rule is a measurable function of those data and its declared independent randomization. Equal input laws give equal pushforward decision laws. A separating intervention or observation, or an independently justified identifying restriction, is necessary to escape this obstruction.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{What a failure refutes.}
A valid rejection challenges the conjunction of the model's structural prediction, its realization map, the observation/calibration contract and its causal assumptions. It does not refute an abstract mathematical theorem whose hypotheses were never verified in the preparation. A failed sham, lost measurement channel, broken blinding or unidentified mapping yields invalid or insufficient evidence according to the frozen rules. No empirical biological dataset or completed experiment is supplied here; external support remains uncertified.


\section{Acceptance Criteria}
\begin{criterion}[Intermediate Claim Gate]
Support at intermediate tier requires:
\begin{enumerate}
\item at least $3/5$ predictions supported,
\item each supported prediction beats at least 3 comparators,
\item zero severe forensic violations in QICN FULL.
\end{enumerate}
\end{criterion}

\begin{criterion}[Latency/Tick Budget Invalidation]
If runtime exceeds budget envelope (e.g., persistent $p99$ overflow above pre-registered tolerance), the corresponding runs are invalid for cognitive-operational claims.
\end{criterion}

\begin{criterion}[Escalation Block]
A result cannot move beyond methodological readiness if any of the following hold:
\begin{enumerate}
\item a primary endpoint is computed from packets that failed integrity;
\item a comparator has no declared coverage vector;
\item negative controls are absent for the claim family;
\item the practical irrelevance band was chosen after looking at outcomes;
\item exclusion counts cannot be reconstructed from the artifact set.
\end{enumerate}
\end{criterion}

\subsection{Claim Classes Produced by the Protocol}
The protocol distinguishes four output classes:
\begin{enumerate}
\item \texttt{\detokenize{PIPELINE_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{READY}}: artifacts and gates work, but evidence is pilot or
synthetic.
\item \texttt{\detokenize{INTERNAL_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{COMPARAT}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{IVE_}\allowbreak{}\detokenize{SUPPORT}}: frozen internal runs support a
bounded contrast against declared comparators.
\item \texttt{WEAKENED}: one or more primary contrasts fail or become
inconclusive.
\item \texttt{INVALID}: integrity, eligibility, or traceability fails before
scientific interpretation.
\end{enumerate}
Only the second class can support a bounded internal scientific reading, and even
there the support is limited to the declared systems, scenarios, seeds, and gates.

