\]
Then $\|w\|=\sqrt{2/3}<1$, $\|\ell\|=\sqrt{3/2}<2$, and $|\ell(z)|\le1$ on $Q$. Let $B=\{0,1\}^{\mathbb N_{>0}}$, $\beta=1/3$, $\lambda=1/32$, and $A=\mathbb T\times B\times Q$. With $R_*(b)=(b_1,0,1-b_2,1-b_3,\ldots)$, the controls are
\[
 D_{u,q,v}(\theta,b,z)=
 \left(u+q\lambda\bigl(b_1+\ell(z)/4\bigr),\ R_*(b),\
 (z+v)/4+w\cos(2\pi\theta)/32\right),
\]
where $u\in\mathbb T$, $q\in\{-1,0,1\}$ and $v\in Q$, together with $W(\theta,b,z)=(\theta,(1-b_1,b_2,\ldots),z)$. Angles have circumference one. The fixed channels are
\[
 r_0=\theta,\qquad r_1=\theta,\qquad
 c=(c_B(b),z/16),\quad c_B(b)=\sum_{j\ge1}2b_j3^{-j}.
\]
Observation error is the maximum of $\rho(r_0,r'_0)$, $\beta\rho(r_1,r'_1)$, $|c_B-c'_B|$ and $\|z-z'\|/16$. The product sector is the same full split-channel sector used above: every fixed homeomorphism onto a product of two non-singleton compact factors, every nonempty partition of these three fixed channels, arbitrary time-homogeneous local dynamics with common exogenous controls, and no joint output map. Error is a supremum over all initial states, controls and times, including zero and one. No restriction to a single coordinate product or a fitted rival is made.

The causal object $C$ is the specified structural graph: every Hilbert coordinate and $b_1$ enters the phase update; phase and each coordinate's own past enter its Hilbert update; the binary tail has the displayed shift edges. This is the graph of the defined equations, not a causal inference from observational data. It also records the fixed channel and comparison structure. A normalized actuation cost is $E_{\rm ctl}(D_{u,q,v})=1+\|v\|^2\le5/3$ and $E_{\rm ctl}(W)=1$. Actuation cost, the resistance-sector energy $E_w$ and the weighted bonding-map defect $D_w$ are distinct data; no equality is assumed. The admissible controls are mathematically ideal continuous inputs; finite laboratory precision is not asserted.

The identity presentation is the cofinal prefix diagram with finite boxes for the first $n$ Hilbert coordinate pairs, together with the circle and first $n$ bits. Its predeclared alternatives are the two angular bonding presentations $I_0,I_{1/4}$, with the single jump at depth one and weight $\beta$. Hilbert and bit coordinates are transported identically. The target is $I_0$. This is a separated presentation family, not the continuous family of arbitrarily small jumps.
The native resistance sector changes the control law while retaining this bonding diagram and full admissible initial-state support. Its structural identity set is $I_0$, not the one-step image $D(A)$. Thus its bonding-map defect and identity-set deformation are zero; the positive-deformation resistance infimum is empty and $M_\Omega=+\infty$ in that sector. The alternative jump presentation is a comparison model, not a reachable native change of the diagram. This convention does not supply causal integration or product atomicity; those require the separate bounds below.

For non-collapse use the Hilbert embedding with first five coordinates
\[
 (1/2+\cos(2\pi\theta)/2,\ 1/2+\sin(2\pi\theta)/2,
  1/2+\cos(2\pi\theta)/2,\ 1/2+\sin(2\pi\theta)/2,\ b_1),
\]
followed by the weighted binary tail and $z$. The first five basis vectors can be chosen as normalized indicators of five equal-measure cells in $L^2[0,1]$; complete them by an orthonormal basis. Thus constant profiles have equal first five coefficients. In this ambient Hilbert space set $\gamma(x)=\operatorname{dist}(x,A)$, so $X_\Gamma=A$. The constant-profile set is contained in the closed subspace where coordinates 1, 2 and 5 agree. All transitions extend continuously to the ambient Hilbert space: the new angular coordinates are trigonometric functions of the displayed scalar, the old cosine in the Hilbert update is $2x_1-1$, and the weighted binary shift is affine. Phase readouts off their circle are given any fixed measurable convention at the centre. The bit reading extends to all ambient states by clipping each recovered bit to $[0,1]$ before its summable weighted sum; this is a uniformly convergent series of continuous functions. The Hilbert readout extends linearly with codomain $H$. These choices make all readout maps total; certificates use their restrictions to $A$.

The legibility classes are $b_1\in\{0,1\}$. Sham schedules use $D_{0,0,0}$; a target applies $W$ once and then sham. Use threshold $1/2$, noise radius $1/16$, duration any fixed $T\ge1$, and a one-bit class encoder. Regime assignment is $\Pi=(c_B,z/16)$ into the complete convex space $[0,1]\times(Q/16)$, with its maximum metric and its single compatibility component. Time is discrete. Its affine observation interpolation, when used, is not a continuous-time trajectory in the binary state space.
