\subsection{Discriminator Strength and Identifiability}
A discriminator is useful only when a positive or negative outcome changes the
status of at least one rival explanation. The framework therefore treats
discriminators as identifiable contrasts, not as loose examples.

\begin{definition}[Identifiable Discriminator]
A discriminator $D$ for claim $C$ is identifiable when it specifies:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item an observable decision variable $Y_D$;
\item a manipulation or matched comparison $M_D$;
\item a framework-side expected region $R_Q$;
\item a rival-side expected region $R_R$;
\item a destruction condition $F_D$ under which the claim is weakened or rejected.
\end{enumerate}
The discriminator is strong when $R_Q\cap R_R$ is small under the declared
measurement tolerance.
\end{definition}

\begin{criterion}[Discriminator Usefulness]
A test does not materially discriminate the framework from a rival if both the
framework and the rival predict the same admissible region for the tested
observable. Such a test can still debug the pipeline, but it cannot upgrade a
claim.
\end{criterion}

\begin{proposition}[Near-Miss Value]
Near-miss cases carry more diagnostic value than easy positive cases when they
are constructed by perturbing one binding coordinate while holding gross
complexity, task exposure, and observability approximately fixed.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
If a case is far from the decision boundary, many rival accounts can agree on the
outcome. A near-miss case forces the judge to expose which coordinate controls
the transition. Holding gross complexity and task exposure approximately fixed
reduces the chance that the result is explained by scale, generic performance, or
obvious incompetence rather than by the claimed invariant structure.
\end{proof}

