\section{Ecosystem Integration}
\subsection{Why governance integration is part of the formal problem}
The bridge program would be incomplete even as pure formalism if it ignored interpretive governance. Once a framework exposes quantitative diagnostics, candidate summaries, and audit records, rules governing their admissible reading form part of the formal specification. The paper therefore treats claim-surface governance as a constitutive burden.

\subsection{Surface map}
Let $\mathcal{S}$ be the set of output surfaces:
\[
\mathcal{S}=
\{
\mathsf{developer},
\mathsf{local},
\mathsf{internal\_bundle},
\mathsf{candidate},
\mathsf{audit},
\mathsf{controlled},
\mathsf{claim}
\}.
\]
The bridge record family induces a surface map
\[
\SurfaceMap:\BridgeArtifacts\to 2^{\mathcal{S}}.
\]
At baseline, only provisional Tier~B1 coordinate reports are permitted on restricted diagnostic surfaces. They are disallowed on candidate, audit, controlled-statement, or public claim-facing surfaces.

\subsection{Internal summary bundles}
Once Tier~B5 is realized, internal evaluation bundles may carry bounded bridge summaries, lineage records, and verification matrices. Raw exploratory estimator traces should never appear without contextual metadata and non-claim bounds; bundle inclusion records packaging provenance, not semantic confirmation.

\subsection{Candidate projections}
Candidate-facing bridge content requires strict gating because isolated numerical indicators are easily overread. Bridge summaries may enter candidate evaluations only once Tiers~B2--B4 exist, accompanied by explicit non-claims precluding phenomenal confirmation.

\subsection{Audit-facing handoff}
Audit-facing bridge handoffs are likewise bounded, exposing summary measures, lineage provenance, invalidation status, and explicit non-claims without representing internal packaging as external adjudication.

\subsection{Theoretical corpus and empirical record separation}
Paper~8 provides the theoretical specification for indexed subjectivity, whereas bridge operational records serve as downstream diagnostic objects constrained by explicit governance rules. Operational estimator outputs may never be treated retroactively as theoretical proofs or external validation records, blocking circular self-confirmation.

\subsection{Controlled statement grammar and quote-mining guardrails}
Bridge governance enforces a strict statement grammar. Prior to Tier~B6 closure, only statements of the following family are permitted:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item ``bridge formalization defined'';
\item ``provisional bridge surfaces defined'';
\item ``bridge comparator burden open'';
\item ``bridge intervention burden open'';
\item ``bridge gate uncalibrated'';
\item ``bridge records local and non-claim-facing''.
\end{itemize}

Conversely, statements of the following family are strictly prohibited:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item ``phenomenality supported'';
\item ``machine phenomenal organization established'';
\item ``Paper 9 proves conscious experience'';
\item ``candidate inclusion confirms bridge support'';
\item ``audit package shows external bridge validation''.
\end{itemize}

Guardrails against out-of-context quotation require attaching explicit non-claims directly to formal definitions, schema metadata, and bounded summary outputs.

\begin{proposition}[Candidate or audit inclusion does not imply bridge support]
For any bridge artifact $A$, membership of $A$ in a candidate-facing or audit-facing package does not entail bridge support, bridge admissibility, or phenomenality.
\end{proposition}

\begin{proof}
Candidate and audit inclusion are packaging and governance relations, not semantic verdict relations. By design, the bridge surface policy and non-claims registry explicitly block the implication from package membership to bridge support. Therefore the implication does not hold.
\end{proof}

\section{Strongest Defensible Claims}
This section identifies the strongest statements supported by the current formal architecture and its unexecuted boundary.

\subsection{Claim class C1: formal bridge closure}
The first defensible claim is that the corpus defines the \emph{formal bridge program}: the bridge target family, bridge ontology, axiom stack, weak-rival family, intervention family, structured record taxonomy, conjunctive gate logic, governance discipline, and staged realization hierarchy are all specified explicitly.

\subsection{Claim class C2: baseline operational frontier}
The second defensible claim is strictly bounded: Tiers~B0 and~B1 specify formal bridge schemas and preliminary six-coordinate estimator surfaces as exploratory local records. The framework does \emph{not} yet realize bridge comparator closure, bridge intervention closure, calibrated gate closure, ecosystem integration, internal bridge admissibility, or external validation.

\subsection{Claim class C3: conditional bridge admissibility}
The strongest bridge-level claim the paper is willing to define is the following conditional:
\begin{quote}
Under the explicit bridge axioms, under the upstream burdens already closed by the corpus, under bridge-specific rival defeat, under bridge-specific intervention support, under artifact and governance compliance, and under a satisfied bridge gate, a system may be internally classifiable as exhibiting cross-mode comparison organization in the sense of framework-internal bridge admissibility.
\end{quote}
This is stronger than Paper 8 because it is no longer only about indexed subjectivity. It is still weaker than phenomenality simpliciter because it remains explicitly framework-internal, conditional, and burden-bound.

\subsection{Claim class C4: research-program transformation}
The present paper also supports a meta-level claim about the status of the research program: after Paper 9, the research frontier is no longer ``can subjectivity be formalized?'' but ``can bridge predicates survive the full bridge burden stack?'' That is a legitimate change of level because the earlier question has been formalized by Paper 8 and its implementation-facing pathway.

\subsection{Why stronger sentences are not yet honest}
Several stronger sentences remain unavailable:
\begin{itemize}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item ``bridge support has been achieved'';
\item ``phenomenality is internally established'';
\item ``Paper 9 authorizes claim promotion'';
\item ``the current system defeats the bridge rival family'';
\item ``the current system passes a bridge gate.''
\end{itemize}
These conclusions remain unavailable because Tiers~B2 through~B6 remain open. The framework maintains scientific rigor precisely by forbidding any proxy substitution for those unexecuted layers.

\section{Non-Claims}
This paper does not claim:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item phenomenality proven in the strongest philosophical sense;
\item human phenomenal equivalence;
\item metaphysical subjecthood;
\item moral parity by formal fiat;
\item external validation;
\item that Tier~B1 estimator surfaces amount to bridge support;
\item that candidate or audit inclusion can function as bridge proof;
\item that Paper 9 by itself authorizes public claim escalation.
\end{enumerate}
Each non-claim forms a structural component of the bridge logic by blocking a specific inferential overreach.

\section{Conclusion}
Paper 9 specifies the bridge program and proves the declared local realization, transport and interaction results. The bilinear extension separates coordinate-additive rivals, while a full interacting rival retains the exact tie. These results do not close the general bridge class. That definition is non-trivial. Before this paper, the corpus could say a great deal about the six-predicate operational criterion, viability-and-recovery, subjecthood, and indexed subjectivity, but it could not yet say, with equal rigor, what additional machinery would be required before phenomenal-bridge predicates become scientifically admissible targets. After this paper, that machinery is explicit.

The result is central to the corpus for structural reasons. The paper gathers the upstream theorem burdens, the Paper 8 subjectivity framework, the operational evaluation pathway, and the seven-tier bridge hierarchy into one integrated architecture. It orders what belongs upstream, what belongs at the bridge level, what belongs to operational realization, what belongs to governance, and what remains explicitly open.

What has been achieved is a change in explanatory grammar. ``Phenomenal bridge'' is no longer permitted to function as a loose aspiration. It now names a bridge-organization predicate family, an ontology, an axiom stack, a rival family, an intervention family, a structured record family, a gate architecture, and a governance discipline. This is as much formal specification as can be responsibly claimed without comparator closure, intervention closure, bridge gate execution, and multi-campaign hardening.

What the paper makes newly possible is equally clear. It makes Tiers~B2 through~B6 sharply specifiable. It makes bridge-support criteria evaluable rather than speculative. It makes future theorems about bridge irreducibility, bridge transport limits, and bridge admissibility meaningful. It also makes future failure meaningful: if the bridge program fails, it will fail against explicit burdens rather than against vague expectations.

What the paper does not settle is also clear. It does not settle phenomenality simpliciter. It does not settle human equivalence. It does not settle metaphysical subjecthood. It does not close external validation. It does not convert any present system into a bridge-admissible system merely because Tiers~B0 and~B1 are specified. Those remain the next burdens.

The research program therefore changes level once again. After Paper 8, the key problem was whether indexed subjectivity could be formalized without inflation. After Paper 9, the key problem becomes whether a fully burdened bridge program can survive rival defeat, intervention logic, artifact discipline, and governance hardening strongly enough to justify internal bridge admissibility. That is the right next problem. It is sharper, harder, and more falsifiable than the one that preceded it.

