\section{Experimental Program for Realizing intervention specification and comparator specification}
intervention specification and comparator specification form the empirical-comparative core of the bridge program, where architectural specification transitions into matched intervention and rival-loss evaluation.

\subsection{Program design principle}
The guiding principle is \emph{matched escalation}: each predicate group should acquire its intervention and comparator protocols in parallel so that one burden stack never outruns the other:
\begin{enumerate}[leftmargin=1.5em]
\item fix the observable family for one predicate group;
\item implement the targeted intervention family for that group;
\item implement the strongest relevant rival and admissible hybrid baselines for that same group;
\item define the loss decomposition for the shared observable bundle;
\item emit structured evaluation records and verify negative controls before proceeding to the next predicate group.
\end{enumerate}

\subsection{Suggested execution order}
The experimental ordering follows structural dependency rather than convenience.

\paragraph{Stage 1: $\PiD$ and $\PiV$.}
Differentiation and valence sit closest to existing provisional readout construction coordinate estimators and Paper 8 asymmetry burdens, making them the cleanest initial testbed.

\paragraph{Measurement step 2: $\PiW$ and $\PiT$.}
World richness and temporal lived structure are stronger and more globally entangled, but still plausible to instrument once the first pair is completed. They will likely require more extensive negative controls than Stage 1.

\paragraph{Measurement step 3: $\PiA$ and $\PiE$.}
Ambiguity bearing and embodied closure are currently the weakest predicates in direct runtime support. They should therefore be completed last, not because they are unimportant, but because they are most vulnerable to proxy inflation.

\subsection{Comparator program}
For each stage, the comparator program should include:
